Revision 14, after D131 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-first review, verify-66, and T03's twentieth, verify-67; the owner had answered revision 13's questions as D125 to D127). R4 and R42 change; their ids, forms and risk tags do not. R4 now states a second exception to its longer-phrase clause, beside the bank's code: cash paid-in wording is never taken in by a payment-method cue's longer listed phrase. So with FPS CASH DEPOSIT listed as a payment-method phrase, FPS CASH DEPOSIT is ambiguous, a payment rail with cash paid-in wording, not a payment rail alone (D127). R4 also says that no rule of the maintained lists carries an exception, an unless or a requires: the system refuses to load such a table, naming the rule, because an exception could hide one list's wording from the ambiguity check (D126); the frozen v1 table is as R54 says. R42 now says when a save is told it was recorded: only once, after its head record is published, it reads back an intact log that holds its entry where that head record lists it; otherwise the save is reported as not recorded. R42 also says what a repair re-checks. Immediately before it renames the repaired log over the log, it re-checks that the log and the head record are as its check read them; immediately before it renames its head record into place, that the head record is; and it refuses a rename otherwise (D125). D131 says 'before each rename, that the log and head record are as its check read them'. Before a repair's second rename the log is the one its own first rename published, so the log cannot then be as the check read it. R42 therefore names what each rename re-checks, as D125's record of what T12 built does, and the save's read-back checks the log after. Every other requirement, property, term and id is as revision 13 left it.

Revision 13, after D124 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twentieth review, verify-64, which D124 calls verify-65). R42 and P15 change; their ids, forms and risk tags do not. R42 now runs one save at a time per holder: a save begun inside another save on the same thread, from a signal handler, a finaliser or a callback, is refused before it writes anything, and a save is refused when the log grew since its check. Its scope now states the repair boundary that D103 left to the design. A writer refused after its line reports its save as not recorded, and its entry stays past the recorded entries, as a crash leaves it; the next holder's repair, once a person confirms it, may remove that entry, and an intact log without it is within the guarantee, since no recorded save was lost. Under lock-file interference, if two writers are each told that a save at the same log position was recorded, the log is reported broken, never intact, and a log that reads intact holds the entry of every save whose writer was told it was recorded. This replaces revision 10's 'if such interference still lets two writers append', which verify-64 showed a repair can defeat: A, refused after its line, and B, which dropped A's entry and appended, both appended, and the log read intact. D124 says 'when two writers were each told their save was recorded'; R42 adds 'a save at the same log position', because two saves told recorded one after the other, at positions 2 and 3, leave an intact log that holds both, and nothing is wrong with it. P15 says the same. Two terms, save and repair, are new. Every other requirement, property, term and id is as revision 12 left it.

Revision 12, after D112 (changes requested; raised as D108 and D109 under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's verify-55). R42, R43 and P15 change; their ids, forms and risk tags do not. R42's scope now names the steps a writer re-checks its lock before: each step that changes a file a reader reads, which are the empty head record written before the first entry's line, each entry's line, and each rename that publishes a head record or a repaired log. Writing a temporary file that nobody reads is not such a step (D108). R43 and P15 now say when a steady read gives the recorded entries: only when one writer held the case through it, which a reader knows when it held the gate through the read. A reader that found the case lock free and held it shared knows that no writer could take the case during its read, and judges the whole log. When the gate is busy and the case lock is held, a reader cannot know that one writer held the case through its read: from a steady read it names a change, removal or reordering at or before the recorded entries, and otherwise it claims nothing, so the case is being saved with nothing claimed (D109). P15 now says 'any reader that finds the case free', as R43 does, where it said 'any reader of a free case', so that a reader in that corner, which never finds the case free, is not taken for a free case's reader that fails to name an incomplete entry. One term, steady read, is new. Every other requirement, property, term and id is as revision 11 left it.

Revision 11, after D106 (changes requested; raised as D101 and D102 under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T03's verify-53). Only R4 changes; its id, form and risk tag do not. A table edit could make two of R4's clauses meet, and R4 now says what happens then. A returned-payment code the bank prints at the start of the line settles the line type before the longer-phrase rule applies. It never counts as inside another rule's longer listed phrase, so with UNPAID DD TO SAVINGS on an own-pot list, UNPAID DD TO SAVINGS is still a returned payment (D101). And a match's length is the longest that any alternative of the rule's pattern or names can make where the match starts. The order of alternatives inside a rule therefore picks nothing, just as rule order picks nothing (D102). Every other requirement, property, term and id is as revision 10 left it.

Revision 10, after D99 (changes requested; raised as D98 under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after the thirteenth T12 review, verify-52). R42 and P15 state one more part of the case log's scope; their ids, forms and risk tags do not change. The case lock files, the gate and the case lock beside the log, are the tool's own. Removing or replacing either while the case is in use is outside R42's single-writer guarantee, as hand-editing the log is. A writer re-checks its lock before each write and refuses the write if the case lock file was removed or replaced, and if such interference still lets two writers append, the log they leave is reported broken (R43), never intact. One term, case lock files, is new. Every other requirement, property, term and id is as revision 9 left it.

Revision 9, after D88 (changes requested; the owner raised it as D86 after the tenth T03 review, verify-44). R5 and R6 change; their ids, forms and risk tags do not. A credit whose counterparty matches both a director or shareholder named in the profile and one of the applicant's other accounts named in the profile, and that no protected verdict decides, is ambiguous whether or not the rule table finds its wording ambiguous. It goes to unexplained money in, with every rule it matched in either stage and both kinds of case-profile name as its candidates, and only a per-line override (R17) or an own-account pairing (R8) settles it; a firm rule does not. Until this revision R5 came before R6, so such a credit was director funding without anyone seeing the choice. R5 and R6 state the rule in the same words. Its candidates are R4's term as revision 7 left it, so a reading the profile names outranked, such as a gateway's name, stays in view. D88 also says the credit goes to the judgement queue. R15 already lists every unexplained line and shows each ambiguous credit's candidates with their buckets, so R5 and R6 point to R15 rather than require the queue themselves, as D58 and D60 did for R4: T05, which builds R5 and R6, does not build the queue. A debit is never ambiguous; R5 speaks only of credits, so R6 on a debit is unchanged. Every other requirement, property, term and id is as revision 8 left it.

Revision 8, after D82 (changes requested; the owner answered revision 7's question as D81 and asked for the answer in this revision as D82). R5 and R6 change; their ids, forms and risk tags do not. Each now says that a protected verdict decides a line before a case-profile name does, so REFUND JANE DOE is a refund and JANE DOE LOAN a lending drawdown even with Jane Doe named as a director. This is the precedence the design has held since revision 3 and T03 builds and tests (tests/test_rules_v2.py:335-346, 396-449 and 467 in the T03 worktree). R5 speaks only of credits. R6 speaks of lines in either direction, so its exception does too: a debit to a lender, or a returned direct debit, that names an own account keeps its protected verdict, as T03 builds. One term, protected verdict, is new and names what D82 lists. Every other requirement, property and id is as revision 7 left it.

Revision 7, after D79 (changes requested; the owner raised it as D78 and D76 after the ninth T03 review, verify-41). R4, R5 and R6 change; their ids, forms and risk tags do not. In R4, only a per-line override (R17) or an own-account pairing (R8) settles an ambiguous credit, and a firm rule still does not. A case-profile name (R5, R6) that the credit's wording also matches adds its bucket as one more candidate and does not settle it, because the description cannot reliably tell the payer from a payment reference that names someone else. R5 and R6 still decide every credit the table does not find ambiguous, and say the rest only as far as they must. Cheque wording is named beside the payment rail as a payment-method cue with no bucket (D70, D76), so a cheque with cash paid-in wording is ambiguous by R4's own text. Because a payment-method cue points to no bucket, R4's names clause now applies 'when no line-type cue points to a money-in bucket' instead of 'with no line-type cue'; read the old way, the rail on every Faster Payment, and now a cheque, would keep two disagreeing name lists from making a credit ambiguous, which is not what the design has said since revision 3 or what T03 builds. The terms line-type cue and candidates are widened, and one term, payment-method cue, is new. Every other requirement, property and id is as revision 6 left it.

Revision 6, after D74 (changes requested; the owner raised it as D73 after T12's latest review). R43 and P15 get a simpler reader rule, and the term append in progress, defined in revision 5, gives way to two terms, recorded entries and being saved; the ids, forms and risk tags of R43 and P15 do not change. While another case-log writer holds the case, a reader judges only the recorded entries, from a read across which the head record did not change: it reports a change, removal or reordering at or before them as broken, naming the entry, and anything after them as being saved, never as intact and never as broken. With no such read within its retries, the reader reports the case as being saved and claims nothing about any entry. Whoever holds the case, sign-off included, and any reader of a free case report an incomplete last entry as incomplete and name it. A lock the reader's own thread holds through another CaseLog object or path, and a lock that cannot be taken or tested, are not another holder. This supersedes D67's distinction between a holder that appends and one that only checks. Every other requirement, property and id is as revision 5 left it.

Revision 5, after D65 (changes requested; the owner raised it as D63 after the fifth T12 review). The case log's concurrency scope is stated where R42, R43 and P15 sit; their ids, forms and risk tags do not change. R42 gains one writer at a time per case, across windows, commands, threads and separate CaseLog objects in one process, and its scope: case folders on a local disk, and no fork while the case-log lock is held (the tool never forks), with a forked child's writes through an inherited CaseLog refused. R43 gains what a reader may report: a reader never excuses a torn or tampered log as another writer's append, which this revision reads as excusing nothing except an append in progress while another writer holds the case. P15 states the same scope. Two terms, case-log writer and append in progress, are defined below. Every other requirement, property and id is as revision 4 left it.

Revision 4, after D60 (changes requested; the owner raised it as D58 after the fifth T03 review). R4's queue clause moves to R15, so that each task can complete its own requirement. R4 marks an ambiguous credit as ambiguous, places it in unexplained money in, and records as its candidates every rule it matched in either stage. R15's judgement queue, which already lists every unexplained line, shows each ambiguous credit's candidates and the bucket each would give. One term, candidates, is defined below. Every other requirement, property and id is as revision 3 left it.

Revision 3, after D52 (changes requested; the owner raised it as D49 after the fourth T03 review). Only R4 changes: it gains the ambiguity policy, and the two terms it uses are defined below. Every other requirement, property and id is as revision 2 left it.

Revision 2, after D34 (changes requested). Ids from revision 1 are kept and never reused. Changed: R1 and R14 (cash deposits are trading receipts and the large ones are flagged, D31); R15 (judgement queue); R22, R23, R26 and R28 (the pack is written once, at sign-off, and carries the policy check); R25 (pass/refer/fail shown with the illustrative label, D28); R47 (synthetic cash); R50 (no durations, D32); P1 and P17. New: R55, R56, P19, P20. Withdrawn: R49 (timing the underwriter, D32). Confirmed as designed: local-first (D29), OBIE v3.1 and TrueLayer first (D30), Tier 1 only by 2026-11-29 (D33).

Terms. *The system*: the cma package running on the underwriter's machine. *Figures layer*: src/cma/metrics.py and a new indicators module, the only code that does arithmetic on money (ADR 001). *Case*: one application, holding one or more of the applicant's accounts, a profile, an override log and a case log. *The reply*: the underwriter's written answers of 2026-11-16 (one anecdotal source; see evidence-synthesis.md). *Judgement queue*: the lines the workspace lists first because a person has to decide what they are. *Policy check*: the pass, refer or fail outcome and the rule results from the packaged policy (src/cma/policies/uk_sme_default.yaml). Its rule ids (R1 to R9, cited as [R1] to [R9] in a pack) are not the requirement ids in this file. *Illustrative label*: the sentence shown with every policy outcome, saying that the thresholds ship with the tool, are not the firm's lending policy, and do not make the lending decision. *Preview*: the pack's content and its snapshot hash, shown before sign-off; nothing is written until sign-off. *Line-type cue* (revision 3; widened in revision 7): wording that says what kind of line it is: a returned payment, a move to or from the applicant's own pot or savings, HMRC, a refund or reversal, or a payment-method cue. *Payment-method cue* (revision 7): wording that says how the money came when it did not come as cash: the payment rail (Faster Payment, BACS, CHAPS, a transfer, a standing order) or cheque wording. It is a line-type cue that belongs to no bucket. *Name list* (revision 3): a maintained list of who sent a credit or what it is for: acquirers, gateways, marketplaces, delivery platforms, lenders and merchant cash advance providers, director and capital-introduced wording, cash paid-in wording, invoice references and explained non-trading words. *Candidates* (revision 4; widened in revision 7): every rule an ambiguous credit matched in either stage, that is among its line-type cues and among the name lists, counting a match inside a longer listed phrase only as the longer phrase; and each kind of case-profile name its wording matches, a director or shareholder (R5) and an own account (R6), each giving its own bucket. A payment-method cue can be a candidate; it gives no bucket. *Protected verdict* (revision 8): the category the rule table gives a line whose wording it does not find ambiguous, when the rule that decides is protected: a line-type cue other than a payment-method cue (a returned payment, a move to or from the applicant's own pot or savings, HMRC, a refund or reversal), or the wording of a lender or merchant cash advance provider, a generic loan or advance, a director, owner or shareholder, or capital introduced, and on a debit owner drawings or dividend wording. These are R21's patterns and the line-type cues. A payment-method cue alone gives no verdict, and every protected verdict is non-trading. *Case-log writer* (revision 5): anything that appends to a case's log: a workspace window, a cma command, a thread, or a separate CaseLog object (a handle on the log) in the same process. A case is its folder: two paths to the same folder, a symlink among them, lead to the same log and the same lock. *Recorded entries* (revision 6): the entries the case log's head record, a file kept beside the log, lists as complete. A writer adds its entry to the log and then records it in the head record, so for a moment the log can hold more than the recorded entries. *Being saved* (revision 6): what a reader reports while another case-log writer holds the case: the recorded entries it judged, and that what follows them is not yet checked. It is neither intact nor broken. It replaces revision 5's append in progress. *Case lock files* (revision 10): the two files the tool keeps beside a case's log to order its writers: the gate, which a writer passes alone and briefly to take the case and which a reader holds while it reads, so that no writer takes the case during a read; and the case lock, which the writer holding the case holds. Neither holds data. *Steady read* (revision 12): a read of the case log across which the head record did not change. *Save* (revision 13): one append or one repair by a case-log writer, from its check of the log to the head record that lists its entry; the writer then tells its caller whether the save was recorded. *Repair* (revision 13): dropping an entry past the recorded entries, the torn write that a crash or a refused save left, once a person has confirmed it; the repair records who confirmed it in an entry of its own, so it is a save. *Not confirmed* (revision 17; widened in revision 18): what a save's writer is told when the save's head record may list its entry but the save could not confirm it: the save's read-back failed and its head record was not then taken back, or the rename that published its head record was made and the head record could not then be made durable. It is neither that the save was recorded nor that it was not. The case log, once checked, shows whether the save's entry is among the recorded entries.

## R1 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL place every credit in a case in exactly one money-in bucket (trading receipts, own-account transfers in, director and shareholder funding, lending drawdowns, refunds and returned payments, HMRC repayments, other explained money in, unexplained), and SHALL report total money in and each bucket as a GBP figure with the ids of the lines behind it.
Source: the reply, 'if a tool could reliably tell me that £80k entered the business account but only £55k represented genuine trading receipts, explain the remaining £25k'; D31 (cash deposits sit inside trading receipts, R55).

## R2 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL compute every money figure, bucket, monthly cell, indicator comparison and comparison difference in integer pence from GBP amounts, in the figures layer only, and never in the review workspace, the pack renderer or a model.

## R3 · unwanted · risk: money
IF the money-in buckets do not sum to total money in to the penny, or any credit is in no bucket or in more than one, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT show or export figures for the case, and SHALL report the difference in pence and the lines involved.

## R4 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL recognise, from maintained lists shipped as data: credits from UK card acquirers, payment gateways, marketplaces and delivery platforms as trading receipts; lines naming UK business lenders and merchant cash advance providers as lending drawdowns (money in) or debt service (money out); moves between the applicant's own pots or savings as own-account transfers; HMRC credits as HMRC repayments; refund and reversal credits as refunds; and cash paid in at a counter or machine as cash deposits.
THE SYSTEM SHALL treat a credit's wording as ambiguous when its line-type cues point to more than one money-in bucket, when a payment-method cue (a payment rail or cheque wording) appears with cash paid-in wording, or, when no line-type cue points to a money-in bucket, when the name lists it matches point to more than one money-in bucket; SHALL mark an ambiguous credit as ambiguous, place it in unexplained money in, and record as its candidates every rule it matched in either stage and each kind of case-profile name its wording matches; and SHALL NOT count it as trading receipts unless the underwriter moves it there with an override (R17). Only a per-line override (R17) or an own-account pairing (R8) settles an ambiguous credit; a firm rule does not. A case-profile name (R5, R6) that the credit's wording also matches adds its bucket as one more candidate and does not settle it, because the description cannot reliably tell the payer from a payment reference that names someone else. Line-type cues that agree outrank name lists that point elsewhere (PAYPAL REFUND is a refund). A match inside a longer listed phrase counts only as the longer phrase (STRIPE CAPITAL is a lending drawdown, not also a card settlement; HMRC REFUND is an HMRC repayment), and only a match that itself counts can set another aside inside its longer phrase: a match set aside inside a longer listed phrase sets nothing aside, even when its rule counts by another match elsewhere in the line. Matches are settled from the longest down, so whether a match counts rests only on the longer matches that hold it, and a rule counts, and is among the candidates, where any match of its own counts (with FPS CASH DEPOSIT STRIPE listed as a cash paid-in phrase and FPS CASH DEPOSIT as a gateway's name, the gateway's match is set aside inside the cash phrase and sets nothing aside, and the cash phrase does not take in the rail, so the rail counts and FPS CASH DEPOSIT STRIPE is ambiguous, not a cash deposit; with STRIPE also a gateway's name, FPS CASH DEPOSIT STRIPE | STRIPE is ambiguous too, though the trailing STRIPE keeps the gateway counting, and its candidates are the rail, the cash paid-in rule and the gateway). A match's length is the longest that any alternative of the rule's pattern or names can make where it starts, so the order of alternatives inside a rule picks nothing (with ACME and ACME FASTER PAYMENT LTD as two alternatives of one rule, ACME FASTER PAYMENT LTD matches whole, and the rail inside it does not count). A returned-payment code the bank prints at the start of the line (UNPAID DD) settles the line type on its own, before the longer-phrase rule applies: it never counts as inside another rule's longer listed phrase (with UNPAID DD TO SAVINGS on an own-pot list, UNPAID DD TO SAVINGS is still a returned payment). Cash paid-in wording and a payment-method cue likewise never take each other in: neither counts as inside a longer listed phrase that holds the other, whichever list holds that phrase, so a payment-method cue with cash paid-in wording is always ambiguous (with FPS CASH DEPOSIT listed as a payment-method phrase, FPS CASH DEPOSIT is ambiguous, not a payment-method cue alone; with FPS CASH DEPOSIT listed as a cash paid-in phrase, it is ambiguous, not a cash deposit).
No rule of the maintained lists carries an exception: an unless (the rule does not match where other wording also appears), a requires (it matches only where other wording also appears), or an exception of the rule's own pattern: a lookahead or lookbehind (the pattern tests wording beside its match without matching it), or an anchor, ^, $, \A or \Z (the pattern tests whether anything stands before or after its match in the line). Only the pattern of the bank's returned-payment code (UNPAID DD), which the system lists in its own code, keeps an anchor, its ^ at the start of the line, where the bank prints the code. THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse to load a rule table other than the frozen v1 table (R54) in which any rule carries one, that anchor apart, and SHALL name the rule, because an exception could hide one list's wording from the ambiguity check (with an unless naming FPS on the cash list, FPS CASH DEPOSIT STRIPE would be a card settlement; with the cash rule's pattern written KIOSK CASH DEPOSIT(?! STRIPE), so would FPS KIOSK CASH DEPOSIT STRIPE; and with it written CASH DEPOSIT$, so would FPS CASH DEPOSIT STRIPE). Only the frozen v1 table, as its bytes were verified, is spared this check, and only a digest of the table's bytes that matches the hash the system pins in its own code verifies them: THE SYSTEM SHALL check for exceptions in any table it checks without such a digest, whether no digest is given or another one, whatever the table says of itself, and SHALL name each rule that carries one (with rules that bear v1's id and frozen: true checked with no digest, unless: FPS on one of them is named). The table the system loads as v1 (R54) is that table only if its bytes match the hash the system pins in its own code, and THE SYSTEM SHALL compare them before it checks anything else in the table. With any other bytes, whatever the table says of itself, THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse it as a changed frozen table, naming the table, SHALL name after that whatever else keeps it from loading, and SHALL use none of its rules (with v1's rules list emptied, the refusal says first that v1 has been changed, and then that it has no rules). It SHALL do so whatever the change makes fail, a value the format does not allow included: no failure in loading or checking a changed v1 SHALL reach the caller as itself or be named before the change (with frozen: false set on a copy of v1, and a rule given bank_code: true and a pattern given as a list, the refusal says first that v1 has been changed, and then names that rule's pattern among what else keeps it from loading). A changed v1 is not spared the exception check: among what else keeps it from loading, THE SYSTEM SHALL name each of its rules that carries an exception, naming the rule (with unless: FPS added to a rule of a copy of v1 that keeps frozen: true, the refusal says first that v1 has been changed, and then names that rule's unless). Beyond that check, what else keeps a changed v1 from loading is judged as for the kind of table it says it is: a copy that still calls itself frozen is not held to what only the maintained lists carry, such as a stage on every rule and the category order, which v1's own rules lack, and a copy that does not is checked as the maintained lists are. Every table the system does not load as v1, whether or not it calls itself frozen or bears v1's id, is checked rule by rule as the maintained lists are, and THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse it, naming the rule, where a rule carries an exception (with frozen: true set on a copy of the v2 table whose cash rule's pattern is CASH DEPOSIT$, the refusal names the cash rule). A value that the table format does not allow in a rule's field, such as a pattern given as a list, a mapping or a number, is a fault of that rule, whatever else the rule carries, the bank-code flag included, and wherever the table's checks read the value: THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse the table naming the rule and the field, with the table's other faults, and SHALL NOT raise it as a type error (with the cash rule's pattern given as the list [CASH DEPOSIT] and unless: FPS on the same rule, the refusal names both of the cash rule's faults; with the returned-payment rule's pattern given as the list [UNPAID DD] beside its bank_code: true, and unless: FPS on the cash rule, the refusal names, among the table's faults, the returned-payment rule's pattern and the cash rule's unless). THE SYSTEM SHALL build every rule's matchers, in every form it uses to match a line, while it checks the table, and a pattern that compiles on its own but cannot be used in one of those forms is likewise a fault of its rule: THE SYSTEM SHALL name it, by its rule, with the table's other faults, whatever else the rule carries, and never only once they are mended (with cash_paid_in's names replaced by the pattern (?i)CASH DEPOSIT, whose flag no longer stands at the start once the system wraps the pattern, and unless: FPS on another rule, the refusal names both). A key given more than once, in the table or in a rule, is a fault, named with the table's other faults, and THE SYSTEM SHALL still check every value it was given as it checks a key given once, whichever value comes first. With the rules list given more than once, it SHALL check the rules of every list, rule by rule, so that a fault in any of them is named. With a rule's field given more than once, it SHALL check and build each of its values, and SHALL name by its rule a value that does not compile, that compiles on its own but cannot be used in a form the system builds from it, or that carries an exception (with rules: given twice and the cash rule's pattern in the first list written KIOSK CASH DEPOSIT(?! STRIPE), the refusal names the repeated rules key and the cash rule's lookahead; with the cash rule's pattern given twice, first as ( and then as CASH DEPOSIT, it names the repeated pattern and that ( does not compile; with it given first as (?i)CASH DEPOSIT and then as CASH DEPOSIT, it names the repeated pattern and that the first value cannot be used).
Source: README known limits (unknown acquirers land in M16); the reply (Stripe, SumUp, merchant cash advances); D46 (line-type wording before name lists); D48 (bare words are not evidence); D52 (ambiguous or conflicting wording goes to the judgement queue and is never counted as trading receipts); D58 and D60 (R4 marks the credit and records its candidates; the judgement queue that lists it is R15's); D70 and D76 (cheque wording is a payment-method cue with no bucket); D78 and D79 (a profile name adds a candidate and settles no ambiguous credit); D101, D102 and D106 (revision 11: the bank's code settles the line type before the longer-phrase rule; a match is the longest any alternative of the rule can make where it starts); D126, D127 and D131 (revision 14: cash paid-in wording is never taken in by a payment-method cue's longer phrase; no rule of the maintained lists carries an exception); D132 and D136 (revision 15: cash paid-in wording and a payment-method cue never take each other in, whichever list holds the longer phrase; a lookahead or lookbehind in a rule's pattern is an exception); D137 and D141 (revision 16: a v2 pattern carries an anchor only on the bank's returned-payment code; only a match that itself counts can set another aside); D142 and D148 (revision 17: a match set aside sets nothing aside even where its rule counts by another match; matches are settled from the longest down); D153 (revision 18: only the frozen v1 table, known by the hash the system pins, is spared the exception check; every other table, whether or not it calls itself frozen, is checked rule by rule and refused naming the rule); D158 (revision 19: the table the system loads as v1 is judged by its bytes before anything else in it is checked, and what else keeps it from loading is named after that; a value the format does not allow in a rule's field is named by rule, never raised as a type error); D163 (revision 20: a malformed value is named by its rule whatever else the rule carries, the bank-code flag included; whatever a change to v1 makes fail, the refusal names the changed frozen table first); D168 (revision 21: only v1 as its bytes were verified is spared the exception check, so a changed v1 names its exceptions, and is otherwise judged as the kind of table it says it is; every rule's matchers are built while the table is checked, so a pattern that cannot be used is named by its rule with the table's other faults); D192 (revision 22: only a digest of the table's bytes that matches the pin spares v1 the exception check; a key given more than once is a fault, and every value it was given is checked as a key given once is, whichever comes first: the rules of every rules list and each value of a repeated field).

## R5 · conditional · risk: money
IF the case profile names a director or shareholder, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL put each credit whose counterparty matches that name in director and shareholder funding, even when the credit carries an invoice reference, unless a protected verdict decides the credit first; for a credit whose wording the rule table finds ambiguous (R4), or whose counterparty also matches one of the applicant's other accounts named in the profile (R6), the name SHALL instead add director and shareholder funding as one more candidate and SHALL NOT settle it. A protected verdict outranks the name: REFUND JANE DOE is a refund and JANE DOE LOAN a lending drawdown, even with Jane Doe named as a director.
IF a credit's counterparty matches both a director or shareholder and one of the applicant's other accounts named in the profile, and no protected verdict decides the credit, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL treat the credit as ambiguous whether or not the rule table finds its wording ambiguous: it SHALL mark the credit as ambiguous, place it in unexplained money in, and record as its candidates every rule it matched in either stage and both kinds of case-profile name, so that director and shareholder funding and own-account transfers in are both among the buckets they give; and it SHALL NOT count the credit as trading receipts unless the underwriter moves it there with an override (R17). Only a per-line override (R17) or an own-account pairing (R8) settles such a credit; a firm rule does not. The judgement queue lists it, with each candidate's bucket, under R15. With Jane Doe a director and ACME HOLDINGS an own account, FPS CREDIT JANE DOE ACME HOLDINGS is ambiguous, and IWOCA JANE DOE ACME HOLDINGS is a lending drawdown.
Source: D78 and D79 (revision 7: a profile name settles no ambiguous credit); D81 and D82 (revision 8: a protected verdict decides a credit before a case-profile name does); D86 and D88 (revision 9: a credit that a director's and an own account's names both match is ambiguous).

## R6 · conditional · risk: money
IF the case profile names the applicant's other accounts, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL classify lines in either direction whose counterparty matches one of them as own-account transfers, unless a protected verdict decides the line first, and SHALL leave those own-account transfers out of trading receipts and out of operating cash flow (M9); for a credit whose wording the rule table finds ambiguous (R4), or whose counterparty also matches a director or shareholder named in the profile (R5), the name SHALL instead add own-account transfers in as one more candidate and SHALL NOT settle it. A protected verdict outranks the name in either direction: with ACME HOLDINGS named as an own account, a credit that reads IWOCA ACME HOLDINGS is a lending drawdown, and a debit that reads it is debt service.
IF a credit's counterparty matches both a director or shareholder and one of the applicant's other accounts named in the profile, and no protected verdict decides the credit, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL treat the credit as ambiguous whether or not the rule table finds its wording ambiguous: it SHALL mark the credit as ambiguous, place it in unexplained money in, and record as its candidates every rule it matched in either stage and both kinds of case-profile name, so that director and shareholder funding and own-account transfers in are both among the buckets they give; and it SHALL NOT count the credit as trading receipts unless the underwriter moves it there with an override (R17). Only a per-line override (R17) or an own-account pairing (R8) settles such a credit; a firm rule does not. The judgement queue lists it, with each candidate's bucket, under R15. With Jane Doe a director and ACME HOLDINGS an own account, FPS CREDIT JANE DOE ACME HOLDINGS is ambiguous, and IWOCA JANE DOE ACME HOLDINGS is a lending drawdown.
Source: D78 and D79 (revision 7: a profile name settles no ambiguous credit); D81 and D82 (revision 8: a protected verdict decides a line before a case-profile name does); D86 and D88 (revision 9: a credit that a director's and an own account's names both match is ambiguous).

## R7 · unwanted · risk: money
IF no built-in rule, firm rule or override explains a credit, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT count it as trading receipts; it stays in unexplained money in.

## R8 · conditional · risk: money
IF a case holds more than one of the applicant's accounts, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL pair each debit in one account with the single credit of the same amount in another account of the case within the configured number of days, and SHALL classify both lines as own-account transfers.
Source: the reply, 'transfers between business accounts can inflate turnover if counted twice'.

## R9 · unwanted · risk: money
IF a debit has more than one candidate credit for an own-account pairing, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT pair it, and SHALL list the debit and every candidate in the judgement queue as a possible own-account transfer.

## R10 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL keep the ids, units and values of M1 to M16 unchanged on every committed synthetic case and test fixture, except for lines that a new rule now recognises, which the change SHALL list; M16 SHALL be renamed so that its name says it measures money in that is not trading receipts.

## R11 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL produce a monthly summary with one row per calendar month in the period, part months marked as part, giving money in, money out, trading receipts, average end-of-day balance, lowest end-of-day balance and days overdrawn, each cell with the ids of the lines behind it.
Source: the reply, 'calculating monthly credits, debits, average balances and existing repayments'.

## R12 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL report the average end-of-day balance over the period as a figure, citing the lines that set each day's closing balance.

## R13 · conditional · risk: money
IF the case profile gives the proposed facility's monthly repayment, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL report debt service cover including that repayment; otherwise THE SYSTEM SHALL show that figure as n/a with the reason.

## R14 · ubiquitous · risk: data
THE SYSTEM SHALL raise risk indicators from thresholds in an editable file (a single credit that is large against typical trading receipts; each cash deposit at or above the single-deposit limit; a calendar month in which cash deposits exceed a set share of that month's trading receipts; a cash deposit made within a set number of days after a cash withdrawal of an amount within a set tolerance; payments to gambling operators; returned direct debits; runs of consecutive overdrawn days; irregular credits from a named acquirer), each listing its lines and the threshold that fired, and SHALL label any threshold still at its shipped value as illustrative.
Source: the reply, 'Highlight director loans, unusual credits and large cash deposits'; 'Flag returned direct debits, persistent overdraft usage'; D31, 'with deposits above a threshold flagged'.

## R15 · event · risk: ux
WHEN the underwriter opens a case, THE SYSTEM SHALL show the money-in breakdown, the monthly summary, the figures, the indicators and, while R25 applies, the policy check, with the judgement queue (unexplained lines, ambiguous credits among them, possible own-account transfers, and every line an indicator flagged, which includes cash deposits at or above the single-deposit limit) listed first, largest amount first, and SHALL show for each ambiguous credit in the queue its candidates (R4) and the money-in bucket each would give.
Source: D58 and D60 (the queue clause moved here from R4).

## R16 · event · risk: ux
WHEN the underwriter selects a figure, a bucket or a monthly cell, THE SYSTEM SHALL list exactly the lines behind it, each with date, description, amount, balance, category and the rule or override that set the category; for a sum it SHALL show the lines' total next to the figure, and for an average, share, ratio or count it SHALL show the inputs and the method.
Source: the reply, 'I'd want to click any number in the report and see exactly which transactions make up that figure'.

## R17 · event · risk: money
WHEN the underwriter changes a line's category and gives a reason, THE SYSTEM SHALL append the change (line, old and new category, reason, typed name, operating-system user, UTC time) to the case's override log, recompute every figure from the statements and the whole override log, and mark each figure that now rests on an overridden line.
Source: the reply, 'Allow me to manually correct categories'.

## R18 · unwanted · risk: money
IF a requested change would alter a line's amount, date, balance or description, add or remove a line, or give a line a category not valid for its direction, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT accept it; overrides change categories only.

## R19 · conditional · risk: data
IF an override names a line that is not in the case, or was recorded against a statement file whose hash no longer matches, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT apply it, and SHALL show it as stale until the underwriter confirms or drops it.

## R20 · event · risk: data
WHEN the underwriter promotes an override to a firm rule, THE SYSTEM SHALL show the lines the rule would match in the open case before saving, then save the counterparty pattern, direction, category, name, time and reason to the firm rules file, and apply the rule to every case categorised afterwards.

## R21 · unwanted · risk: money
IF a firm rule would reclassify a line matched by a built-in director, capital, shareholder, lender, HMRC or returned-payment pattern, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT apply the firm rule to that line; only a per-line override with a reason can change it.

## R22 · event · risk: data
WHEN the underwriter signs off a case's figures, THE SYSTEM SHALL require a typed name, record it with the operating-system user and UTC time, and bind it to the snapshot of the preview the underwriter signed: statement hashes, ruleset, firm rules, profile, override log position, thresholds, memo field map, policy hash, the show_policy_verdict setting and package version.

## R23 · unwanted · risk: data
IF anything in the snapshot has changed since the preview was built, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT accept the sign-off, and SHALL say what changed.

## R24 · ubiquitous · risk: ux
THE SYSTEM SHALL word the underwriter's action as signing off the figures, never as approving the loan, and SHALL leave the recommendation and every judgement field blank for the underwriter.
Source: the reply, 'I wouldn't want AI making the final lending decision. I'd want it assisting the underwriter.'

## R25 · state · risk: ux
WHILE show_policy_verdict is on in the firm configuration (the default), THE SYSTEM SHALL show in the review workspace and the pack the overall pass, refer or fail outcome and each rule's result from the packaged policy, computed from the current figures, and SHALL put the illustrative label in the same workspace panel and the same pack section as the outcome.
Source: D28, 'Show it, labelled as illustrative thresholds, not the firm's policy'; README:127, 'Policy thresholds are illustrative. They are not any lender's real policy.'

## R26 · event · risk: data
WHEN a case is signed off, THE SYSTEM SHALL write the review pack once, from the signed preview, containing: memo-ready sections ordered by an editable memo field map (by default the 13 fields listed in the reply), each figure followed by its id; while R25 applies, a policy-check section after the memo fields that no memo field maps to; a self-contained HTML record that works offline, with click-through from every figure to its lines and the sign-off; the evidence JSON; and the monthly summary as CSV. THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT overwrite an existing pack.
Source: the reply, 'Produce a summary I can use directly in my credit memo'.

## R27 · conditional · risk: data
IF a memo field has no figure mapped to it, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL mark it as for the underwriter and leave it empty.

## R28 · unwanted · risk: data
IF the pack's text fails the grounding guard (a number that is not a figure or a policy threshold at the precision shown, a figure without its id, or a threshold without its rule id and a limit word), THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT write the pack.

## R29 · state · risk: security
WHILE offline lock is on in the firm configuration (the default), THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse to create a hosted model client, SHALL refuse to start the MCP server, SHALL open no network connection other than the loopback workspace, and SHALL record offline mode in every evidence record.
Source: the reply, 'A tool that operates entirely on our own machine or within our own environment, without transmitting customer data externally, could be attractive.'

## R30 · unwanted · risk: security
IF a hosted model is used, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT put transaction descriptions, counterparty names, case profile fields or any line-level data in a prompt; a prompt carries only the metric and rule tables, the period and the placeholder account name.

## R31 · ubiquitous · risk: security
THE SYSTEM SHALL keep each case's statements, profile, logs and outputs under one case root readable only by the operating-system user who created it, and SHALL refuse to create a case root inside a git working tree that does not ignore it.

## R32 · conditional · risk: security
IF the case root lies inside a folder that a known cloud-sync client uploads (OneDrive, Dropbox, Google Drive, iCloud Drive), THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL warn before creating a case, and SHALL record the warning in the case log.

## R33 · event · risk: security
WHEN the review workspace starts, THE SYSTEM SHALL listen on the loopback interface only, issue a new random session token, refuse any request that lacks the token or carries a Host header other than the loopback address and port, and serve only assets bundled in the package.

## R34 · ubiquitous · risk: security
THE SYSTEM SHALL treat all payer-written and provider-written text (descriptions, references, merchant names, provider categories) as untrusted: escape it in HTML, neutralise leading formula characters in text cells of CSV exports, never send it to a model, and never act on it.

## R35 · event · risk: data
WHEN the underwriter purges a case, THE SYSTEM SHALL delete all the case's files, and SHALL record in a firm-level ledger only the case id, the purge time and who purged it.

## R36 · event · risk: data
WHEN a UK Open Banking (OBIE Account and Transaction API v3.1) transactions file is imported, THE SYSTEM SHALL keep only booked GBP transactions, keep the provider's transaction ids, rebuild the running balance from per-line balances or from a supplied statement balance, and refuse the file if the balance chain breaks anywhere.
Source: D5; D30; E3 and E7 (provider unknown); the reply names TrueLayer, Yapily and Plaid as common.

## R37 · event · risk: data
WHEN a TrueLayer Data API transactions export is imported, THE SYSTEM SHALL apply the same checks as R36, and SHALL keep the provider's own categories only as untrusted hints that no figure uses.
Source: D30.

## R38 · event · risk: data
WHEN a bank CSV with separate paid-in and paid-out columns, a non-ISO date format or newest-first order is imported, THE SYSTEM SHALL convert it through a named bank profile or a custom column mapping, and SHALL apply the same checks as the five-column loader.

## R39 · conditional · risk: data
IF a file already imported into the case is imported again, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL leave the case unchanged.

## R40 · unwanted · risk: data
IF a new account overlaps an existing account of the case in period and shares most of its (date, amount) pairs, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT add it, and SHALL say it is probably the same account imported twice.

## R41 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL keep line ids unique within a case by prefixing them with the account key when the case holds more than one account, compute flow figures across all the case's accounts after removing paired own-account transfers, and compute balance figures on the combined end-of-day balance over the dates every account covers, showing n/a with the reason when those dates do not overlap.

## R42 · ubiquitous · risk: data
THE SYSTEM SHALL append every import, profile change, override, firm-rule promotion, preview, pack write and sign-off to the case log, each entry carrying the hash of the entry before it.
THE SYSTEM SHALL let one case-log writer at a time hold a case, whether the writers are windows, commands, threads or separate CaseLog objects in one process, and SHALL refuse every other writer while one holds it. IF a process forks while it holds a case's lock, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse every write the forked child makes through the CaseLog it inherited.
THE SYSTEM SHALL run one save at a time per holder. IF a save begins inside another save on the same thread (from a signal handler, a finaliser or a callback), THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse it before it writes anything. IF the log grew since a save checked it, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse that save.
THE SYSTEM SHALL tell a save's writer that the save was recorded only once, after the save's head record is published, it has read back an intact log that holds the save's entry at the position that head record lists; otherwise THE SYSTEM SHALL report the save as not recorded or, where this requirement says so, as not confirmed. IF that read-back fails, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL take back the head record the save published, when that head record is still the save's own, by putting back the head record the save's check read, so that the save's entry is past the recorded entries, as a crash leaves it, and SHALL report the save as not recorded; a head record that another writer has published since is not the save's own, and THE SYSTEM SHALL leave it as it is. The take-back's rename publishes a head record, so immediately before it THE SYSTEM SHALL re-check both case lock files, as before every step that changes a file a reader reads (scope below), and that the head record is still the save's own. IF the take-back is not made, because the head record is no longer the save's own, a case lock file was removed or replaced, or the head record cannot be read or renamed, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL report the save as not confirmed, neither recorded nor not recorded, and SHALL ask for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved; the check shows whether the save's entry is among the recorded entries. Immediately before a repair renames the repaired log over the log, THE SYSTEM SHALL re-check that the log and the head record are as the repair's check read them, and immediately before the repair renames its head record into place, that the head record is as that check read it; IF either re-check fails, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse the rename.
IF the file system fails while a save is under way after its check, in a step the save takes (reading, writing, syncing, linking or renaming a file) or in a read it makes to re-check a case lock file, the log or the head record or to read the log back, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL report the save as not recorded, naming the error, unless the rename that publishes the save's head record was made and that head record was not then taken back, in which case THE SYSTEM SHALL report the save as not confirmed, naming the error, and SHALL ask for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved. THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT let the file system's error reach the save's caller as itself. The reads a save makes to check something are among the steps this covers: its comparisons of the log and of the head record with what its check read, before its line and before each rename, a repair's and a take-back's included; its read-back; and its examination of both case lock files before each step that changes a file a reader reads (scope below). A read that cannot be made SHALL be reported as the error it met, and SHALL NOT be answered as a read that finds a change: never as a conflict that asks for the case to be reloaded, and never as a case lock file removed or replaced. A case lock file that is not there when the writer examines it was removed; one that the writer cannot examine for another reason is a read that cannot be made. A re-check that cannot read therefore answers not recorded before the save's head record is published and not confirmed in a take-back, and a read-back that cannot read is a read-back that fails, as below. Whether the rename that publishes the save's head record was made is known from that rename, never inferred from reading the head record afterwards, which may itself be unreadable: a head record that cannot be read in the re-check before its rename stops the rename, and the save answers not recorded, naming the error. So a failure before that rename answers not recorded wherever it falls: at the empty head record written before the first entry's line, the log's opening, a write or sync of the line, the sync of the folder after a new log, a re-check, a repair's read of the log, its staging file, its log rename or the sync after it, or the head record's staging file or its rename. After that rename, a failure in the sync of the folder that holds the head record, which leaves the head record listing the save but not durable, answers not confirmed; a read-back whose read fails is a read-back that fails, taken back as above, and answers not recorded once its take-back is made; and a failure during a take-back, in its re-check, its rename or the sync after it, answers not confirmed, as a take-back that is not made does. Any part of the save's line already written stays past the recorded entries, as a crash leaves it. Once any part of a save's line is in the log, which for a repair is once its repaired log has been renamed over the log, no part of what the save's writer is told, the words of any refusal or error it carries included, SHALL say that nothing was written. That holds whatever the save's answer and whatever stopped it: a refusal under the scope below, a failure of the file system, or a take-back that is not made. Each report gives one answer, and its causes, the words of any refusal or error it carries or is raised from, give no other. No part of a not-recorded report SHALL say that the save was recorded or that it is not confirmed. No part of a not-confirmed report SHALL say that the save was recorded, that it was not recorded, or that nothing was written; where its take-back was not made, it SHALL name what stopped the take-back in the save's own words, as the head record no longer the save's own, a case lock file removed or replaced, or the operating-system error it met, named, and SHALL NOT quote the take-back's refusal.
Scope (revision 5): case folders on a local disk. Forking a process while it holds the case-log lock is not supported, and the tool never forks.
Scope (revision 10; the steps named in revision 12; the repair boundary in revision 13; both lock files in revision 15; the take-back in revision 17; a refusal partway through the line in revision 19; a lock file that cannot be examined in revision 20): the case lock files are the tool's own. Removing or replacing either of them while the case is in use is outside the single-writer guarantee above, as hand-editing the log is. A writer SHALL re-check both case lock files before each step that changes a file a reader reads, the gate it passed to take the case as well as the case lock it holds, and SHALL refuse the step if either of them was removed or replaced; a case lock file it cannot examine is a read that cannot be made, as above, never one removed or replaced. Those steps are the empty head record written before the first entry's line, each entry's line, and each rename that publishes a head record or a repaired log, a take-back's included; writing a temporary file that nobody reads is not such a step. A writer refused once any part of its line is written, and before its head record is published, SHALL report its save as not recorded, never as nothing written in any part of its report (above); the part written, the whole line or less, stays past the recorded entries as a torn entry, as a crash leaves it. A writer whose take-back is refused SHALL report its save as not confirmed, as above. IF such interference lets two writers each be told that a save at the same log position was recorded, THEN the log SHALL be reported broken (R43), never intact; and under such interference a log that reads intact SHALL hold the entry of every save whose writer was told it was recorded. WHEN the next holder's repair, which a person confirmed, removes the entry of a save reported as not recorded or not confirmed, an intact log without that entry is within this guarantee, since no recorded save was lost.
Source: the reply, 'The firm would need confidence that data is secure, access is controlled and there's an audit trail.'; D63 and D65 (the concurrency scope); D98 and D99 (the case lock files are the tool's own); D108 and D112 (revision 12: the steps a writer re-checks its lock before); D124 (revision 13: one save at a time per holder, and D103's repair boundary written into the requirement); D125 and D131 (revision 14: a save is told recorded only after reading back an intact log that holds its entry; a repair re-checks the log and the head record before its renames); D136 (revision 15: the re-check before each step covers the gate as well as the case lock); D141 (revision 16: a save whose read-back fails takes back the head record it published, when that head record is still its own); D143, D148 and D149 (revision 17: a save whose take-back is not made is not confirmed; the take-back's rename stays among the steps a writer re-checks both case lock files before, which D149 keeps); D153 (revision 18: a failure by the file system at a step a save takes after its check answers not recorded before the save's head record's rename and not confirmed after it, naming the error, which never reaches the caller as itself); D154 and D158 (revision 19: every operating-system error a save meets after its check, in a step or in a read it makes to re-check or read back, is reported naming the error, as not recorded, or as not confirmed when the save's head record was published and not taken back; a writer refused once any part of its line is written reports its save as not recorded, never as nothing written); D159 and D163 (revision 20: the reads a save makes to check something are named, the log comparison, the re-checks of the head record and the log, the read-back and the lock-file check; one that cannot be made names its error and never answers as a conflict to reload or a lock file removed or replaced; once any part of a save's line is in the log, no part of its report says nothing was written); D164 and D168 (revision 21: each report gives one answer, its causes included, and a not-confirmed report names what stopped its take-back in the save's own words; whether a save's head record was published is known from its rename, so a head record that cannot be read before its rename answers not recorded).

## R43 · unwanted · risk: data
IF the case log's hash chain is broken or its last entry is incomplete, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT accept a sign-off, and SHALL name the first bad entry. A sign-off decides as a writer holding the case, so it judges the whole log, and an incomplete last entry never passes for a complete one.
IF a reader holds the case, or finds it free, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL report an entry changed, removed or reordered as broken and an incomplete last entry as incomplete, naming the entry.
WHILE another case-log writer holds the case, a reader SHALL judge only the recorded entries, from a steady read; SHALL report a change, removal or reordering at or before them as broken, naming the entry; and SHALL report anything after them as being saved (the recorded entries, and the rest not yet checked), never as intact and never as broken. A steady read SHALL give the recorded entries, and the rest as being saved, only when one writer held the case through it, which the reader knows when it held the gate through the read, so that no other writer could take the case. A reader that found the case lock free and held it shared knows that no writer could take the case during its read, and judges the whole log as a reader that finds the case free.
A reader's read SHALL count only while the lock files it counts on are still the case's lock files. A reader that holds the case, sign-off included, counts on the case lock it holds and the gate it passed to take the case; a reader that finds the gate busy and holds the case lock shared counts on that case lock; and any other reader counts on the gate it holds through the read and on the case lock behind it, whether it holds that case lock shared or finds it held. IF one of them was removed or replaced during the read, THEN the reader SHALL take the read again, as a reader that does not hold the case if it held the case, and SHALL claim nothing from the read across it; IF that happens on every attempt within its retries, THEN the reader SHALL claim nothing about any entry. A read taken again SHALL go through the gate and the case lock, as the reader's first attempt did. A lock file the reader counts on that is missing when the reader compares it after its read was removed during the read; it says nothing about whether a writer has started on the case, so the reader SHALL NOT then read as a reader that finds no gate. A reader that finds the gate busy, holds the case lock shared and finds that case lock removed during its read therefore looks again through the gate and the case lock. A reader learns whether a lock file it counts on was removed or replaced during its read from a check after the read, which compares each lock file it counts on with what that file's path names now. THE SYSTEM SHALL have the check look at each lock file the reader counts on in turn and then, where the reader counts on two, at the first of them again, so that neither is judged only from a look taken before the other's. A reader that finds no case lock file behind the gate counts on the gate alone, and looks after it that there is still no case lock file. A reader that holds the case, sign-off included, SHALL also check that each lock file it holds still has a name, since a file renamed over or removed has none.
Scope (revision 17): beyond a read across a lock file the reader counts on, lock-file interference is outside what a reader can know. A lock file removed or replaced before the reader's read leaves the lock files the reader counts on the case's, while a writer that passed the old gate can still take the case through it, so the reader may report being saved over an entry another writer left incomplete, or report a live append as incomplete. A check made after the interference stops gives the true answer.
Scope (revision 22): a lock-file check is a sequence of looks at the file system, one file at a time, and no look can be joined to the next. A lock file removed or replaced, renamed aside and replaced included, after the check's last look at that file, within the check's own last look, is therefore outside what any check can know, as the window between a writer's check and its step is (R42): the check finds the lock files the case's, and the read stands. A check made after the interference stops gives the true answer.
IF the gate is busy and the case lock is held, so that the reader cannot know that one writer held the case through its read, THEN the reader SHALL name a change, removal or reordering at or before the recorded entries that a steady read shows, naming the entry, and otherwise SHALL claim nothing from that read. IF, within its retries, the reader gets no steady read, or every attempt finds the gate busy and the case lock held with no such fault in a steady read, THEN the reader SHALL report the case as being saved and SHALL claim nothing about any entry.
A lock that the reader's own thread holds through another CaseLog object or another path to the case folder, and a lock that cannot be taken or tested, are not another holder.
Source: D50 (a lock error is not a live writer); D63 and D65 (a reader never excuses a torn or tampered log as another writer's append); D73 and D74 (the reader rule, which supersedes D67's distinction between a holder that appends and one that only checks); D109 and D112 (revision 12: a steady read gives the recorded entries only when one writer held the case through it); D141 (revision 16: a read counts only while the lock files the reader holds are still the case's); D144 and D148 (revision 17: the lock files a holder's read and a busy-gate read count on; lock-file interference beyond a read across them is outside what a reader can know); D153 (revision 18: a read taken again goes through the gate and the case lock; a busy-gate read whose case lock is removed during it looks again); D192 (revision 22: the check after a read looks at each lock file the reader counts on and then at the first again, and a holder also checks that the files it holds still have a name; a lock file removed or replaced after the check's last look at it is outside what any check can know).

## R44 · event · risk: data
WHEN asked to verify an evidence record, THE SYSTEM SHALL recompute every figure, bucket and monthly cell from the recorded inputs and report each mismatch, or confirm that there is none.

## R45 · conditional · risk: data
IF the evidence record was made with a ruleset other than the one installed, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse to verify it, and SHALL name the package version and ruleset that made it.

## R46 · event · risk: data
WHEN asked to pseudonymise a statement, THE SYSTEM SHALL write a copy in which personal and business names and account numbers are replaced by consistent tokens while recognised institutions (acquirers, lenders, HMRC) and all amounts, dates and balances are kept, SHALL tokenise the case profile the same way, and SHALL label the output as pseudonymised personal data, not anonymous data.
Source: the reply, 'I'd start with synthetic or properly anonymised sample statements and compare the results with our existing process'.

## R47 · ubiquitous · risk: none
THE SYSTEM SHALL generate synthetic business cases that include real acquirer names, director funding, own-account transfers across two accounts, cash deposits (at least one at or above the single-deposit limit, and one paid in after a similar cash withdrawal) and lender lines, with ground truth for every bucket, line and indicator, while every existing archetype and profile still produces a byte-identical statement.

## R48 · state · risk: ux
WHILE a case is marked as a demo (synthetic data, or the owner's own statements per D23), THE SYSTEM SHALL show a banner in the workspace and stamp the pack, saying it demonstrates the mechanism and is not an applicant file.

## R50 · event · risk: ux
WHEN asked for a report, THE SYSTEM SHALL summarise for each case the lines in the judgement queue, the lines each indicator flagged, overrides by old and new category, unexplained share of money in, whether every figure was traced, and whether the case was signed off, with no applicant names, descriptions or amounts and no duration derived from the case log's timestamps.
Source: D32 (files are timed with a stopwatch, outside the tool).

## R51 · event · risk: ux
WHEN the underwriter enters their own figure for a memo field or a month, THE SYSTEM SHALL show the difference from its own figure and list, largest first, the lines in that period whose bucket a person might read differently: cash deposits, unexplained lines, possible own-account transfers and overridden lines.
Source: the reply, 'compare the results with our existing process'.

## R52 · event · risk: ux
WHEN the underwriter saves an override on a case of up to 10,000 lines, THE SYSTEM SHALL show the recomputed figures, indicators and policy check within 2 seconds on a typical office laptop.

## R53 · ubiquitous · risk: data
THE SYSTEM SHALL keep cma run, cma resume, cma import monzo, cma generate, the MCP tools and the evidence JSON working with their current options and fields; new evidence fields SHALL be additive, and every new evidence record SHALL carry a schema version.

## R54 · conditional · risk: data
IF CMA_RULESET is set to v1, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL categorise with the built-in rule table as it was before this change, and SHALL record v1 as the ruleset in every evidence record.
IF the table the system loads as v1 has bytes other than those whose hash the system pins in its own code, THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL refuse it as a changed frozen table, naming the table, before it checks anything else in the table, SHALL name after that whatever else keeps it from loading, and SHALL categorise nothing with it (R4).
Source: D153 (revision 18: v1 is known by the hash the system pins); D158 (revision 19: a changed v1 is refused as a changed frozen table before anything else is checked, and what else it breaks is named after that).

## R55 · ubiquitous · risk: money
THE SYSTEM SHALL count every credit categorised as a cash deposit as trading receipts, in M2 and in every figure and monthly cell built on trading receipts, and SHALL report the cash deposits within trading receipts as a separate GBP figure with the ids of the deposits behind it.
Source: D31, 'Counted as trading receipts, with deposits above a threshold flagged'; the reply, 'Highlight ... large cash deposits'.

## R56 · state · risk: ux
WHILE show_policy_verdict is off in the firm configuration, THE SYSTEM SHALL NOT show an overall outcome or any rule result in the review workspace or the pack, and SHALL record in the evidence that the policy check was hidden.
Source: D28 (the setting is the rollback path for showing the outcome).

## P1 · property
For any case, trading receipts and the other seven money-in buckets SHALL sum to total money in to the penny, and every credit's id SHALL appear in exactly one bucket's evidence; the cash-deposits figure is not a bucket and is not added.
Validates: R1, R2, R3, R7

## P2 · property
For any sum figure, bucket or monthly cell, the signed amounts of its evidence lines SHALL total the figure to the penny.
Validates: R1, R11, R16, R55

## P3 · property
For any statements, profile and override log, computing the figures twice SHALL give identical results, and the figures after applying overrides one at a time SHALL equal a fresh computation with all of them applied.
Validates: R17, R44

## P4 · property
For any override log, the set of (id, date, amount, balance, description) across the case's lines SHALL be the same before and after the overrides are applied.
Validates: R18

## P5 · property
For any case whose descriptions and profile contain sentinel strings, no prompt built for a model SHALL contain any sentinel.
Validates: R30, R34

## P6 · property
For any credit that no built-in rule, firm rule or override matches, its id SHALL be in the evidence of unexplained money in and not in the evidence of M2.
Validates: R7

## P7 · property
For any committed synthetic case and test fixture, M1 to M16 under the new ruleset SHALL equal their values before this change, except on lines the change lists as newly recognised; under CMA_RULESET=v1 they SHALL equal them with no exceptions.
Validates: R10, R53, R54

## P8 · property
For any description text, the workspace HTML and the pack HTML SHALL contain it only in escaped form, and no text cell in an exported CSV SHALL begin with =, +, -, @, a tab or a carriage return.
Validates: R34

## P9 · property
For any import file, importing it twice SHALL leave the same case state and the same case log head as importing it once.
Validates: R39

## P10 · property
For any statement and profile, the pseudonymised statement and profile SHALL give identical figures, buckets, monthly cells and indicators.
Validates: R46

## P11 · property
For any multi-account case, each own-account pair SHALL be one debit and one credit of equal amount in different accounts, no line SHALL be in two pairs, and total money in less own-account transfers in SHALL equal the generator's credits from outside the applicant's accounts.
Validates: R6, R8, R9, R41

## P12 · property
For any case, monthly trading receipts SHALL sum to M2 and monthly money in SHALL sum to total money in, to the penny.
Validates: R11, R55

## P13 · property
For any signed pack, verifying its evidence record against its case SHALL reproduce every figure, bucket and monthly cell exactly.
Validates: R22, R44

## P14 · property
For any case, the pack's text SHALL pass the grounding guard with no unmatched numbers and no missing citations.
Validates: R26, R28

## P15 · property
For any case log, read in any window, command or thread and through any CaseLog object or path to the case folder: the holder of the case, sign-off included, and any reader that finds the case free SHALL report any changed, removed or reordered entry as broken and an incomplete last entry as incomplete, naming the entry; while another writer holds the case, a reader SHALL report a change, removal or reordering at or before the recorded entries as broken, naming the entry, judged from a steady read, SHALL report what follows them as being saved, never as intact and never as broken, and SHALL give the recorded entries, and the rest as being saved, only from a steady read through which one writer held the case, which it knows when it held the gate through the read; with the gate busy and the case lock held, it SHALL name only a change, removal or reordering at or before the recorded entries that a steady read shows, and otherwise claim nothing; and with no steady read within its retries, or with every attempt ending in that corner with no such fault, it SHALL claim nothing about any entry. A lock the reader's own thread holds through another CaseLog object or path, and a lock that cannot be taken or tested, are not another holder. A reader's read SHALL count only while the lock files it counts on are still the case's lock files: the case lock it holds and the gate it passed, for the holder of the case, sign-off included; the case lock it holds shared, for a reader that finds the gate busy; and the gate it holds through the read and the case lock behind it, for any other reader. A read across one that was removed or replaced SHALL be taken again, by the holder as a reader that does not hold the case, and nothing SHALL be claimed from it, and with every attempt within the retries read so, nothing SHALL be claimed about any entry. A reader SHALL learn whether a lock file it counts on was removed or replaced during its read from a check after the read that looks at each lock file it counts on and then, where it counts on two, at the first again; the holder of the case, sign-off included, SHALL also check that each lock file it holds still has a name. Lock-file interference beyond a read across a lock file the reader counts on is outside what a reader can know. So, since a lock-file check is a sequence of looks at the file system, is a lock file removed or replaced, renamed aside and replaced included, after the check's last look at that file, within the check's own last look. A check made after the interference stops gives the true answer. For any two case-log writers on one case, whether two processes, two threads sharing one CaseLog object or two CaseLog objects in one process, at most one SHALL hold the case at any moment, and the holder SHALL run one save at a time: a save begun inside another on the same thread SHALL be refused before it writes anything, and a save SHALL be refused when the log grew since its check. Scope: case folders on a local disk; no fork while the case-log lock is held, and a forked child's writes through an inherited CaseLog are refused. Scope (revision 10; the steps named in revision 12; the repair boundary in revision 13; the take-back and its third answer in revision 17; a refusal partway through the line in revision 19; no report of nothing written once the line is in the log in revision 20): the case lock files are the tool's own; removing or replacing either while the case is in use is outside the at-most-one-holder guarantee, as hand-editing the log is. A writer SHALL re-check its lock before each step that changes a file a reader reads (the empty head record written before the first entry's line, each entry's line, and each rename that publishes a head record or a repaired log, a take-back's included; writing a temporary file that nobody reads is not such a step) and SHALL refuse the step if the case lock file was removed or replaced. A writer refused once any part of its line is written, and before its head record is published, SHALL report its save as not recorded, never as nothing written, and the part written, the whole line or less, stays past the recorded entries as a torn entry, as a crash leaves it. Once any part of a save's line is in the log, which for a repair is once its repaired log has been renamed over the log, no part of what the save's writer is told, the words of any refusal or error it carries included, SHALL say that nothing was written, whatever its answer. A save whose read-back fails SHALL take back the head record it published while that head record is still its own, so that its entry stays past the recorded entries and the save is reported as not recorded; a save whose take-back is not made SHALL be reported as not confirmed, neither recorded nor not recorded, and SHALL ask for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved. If such interference lets two writers each be told that a save at the same log position was recorded, the log SHALL be reported broken as R43 says, never intact, and under such interference a log that reads intact SHALL hold the entry of every save whose writer was told it was recorded; a log that lacks only the entry of a save reported as not recorded or not confirmed, which the next holder's repair removed once a person confirmed it, is within the guarantee.
Validates: R42, R43

## P16 · property
For any firm rules file, no line matched by a protected built-in pattern SHALL change category through a firm rule.
Validates: R21

## P17 · property
For any synthetic case generated with the new features, every bucket, every line's category and every indicator flag SHALL equal the generator's ground truth.
Validates: R1, R4, R5, R8, R14, R47, R55

## P18 · property
For any run while offline lock is on, asking for a hosted model or starting the MCP server SHALL fail before any network call is attempted.
Validates: R29

## P19 · property
For any case, the evidence of the cash-deposits figure SHALL be exactly the credits categorised as cash deposits, each of them SHALL also be in M2's evidence, the figure SHALL not exceed M2, and every cash deposit at or above the single-deposit limit SHALL be in the judgement queue with the indicator that flagged it.
Validates: R14, R15, R55

## P20 · property
For any case, with show_policy_verdict on, every workspace panel and pack section that shows a pass, refer or fail outcome SHALL contain the illustrative label and the recommendation field SHALL be empty; with it off, neither the workspace nor the pack SHALL contain an outcome or a rule result.
Validates: R24, R25, R56

