7:Revision 20, 2026-11-20, after D163 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-seventh review, verify-78, and T03's twenty-sixth, verify-79). What changed from revision 19, keeping every id. D159 answered revision 19's question with its default: the reads a save makes only to check something name an operating-system error, and a re-check that cannot read answers not recorded, never a conflict; the owner built it in 1e35352. R42 now names those reads: the comparisons of the log and the head record with the save's check, the read-back and the examination of the case lock files. It says that one that cannot be made is reported as its error, never as a conflict to reload or a lock file removed or replaced (D163). R42 and P15 now say that once any part of a save's line is in the log, no part of what its writer is told says nothing was written (D163, after verify-78; de6a56e). R4 now says that a malformed value is a fault of its rule whatever else the rule carries, the bank-code flag included. It also says that whatever a change to v1 makes fail, the refusal names the changed frozen table first (D163, after verify-79; 5139e22). No other requirement changes; R54 already says what R4 now adds for a changed v1. The owner built these before this revision, and none is reviewed yet: T12's de6a56e and 1e35352, and T03's 5139e22. At 1e35352 a save whose take-back is refused quotes the refusal in its not-confirmed report. With a case lock file replaced, the quoted words say nothing was written, which R42 and P15 now rule out. With the head record no longer the save's own, they say the save was not recorded, so revision 20 asks the owner whether a not-confirmed report may quote what stopped its take-back (Options considered). At 5139e22 no type error escapes the table checks, but a failure no check foresees is refused naming no rule (Context, T03's twenty-sixth review). The design takes in verify-78 and verify-79 and D159 to D163. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, C5, the traceability table, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 20, 23 and 24.
9:Revision 19, 2026-11-20, after D158 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-sixth review, verify-76, and T03's twenty-fifth, verify-77). What changed from revision 18, keeping every id. D154 answered revision 18's question with its default: R42's answer for a save the file system fails reaches every step the save takes after its check, and 51c8b68 maps the steps that change a file. R42 now states that answer as one rule: every operating-system error a save meets after its check is reported, naming the error, as not recorded, or as not confirmed when the save's head record was published and not taken back (D158). It names the steps the rule covers, and counts among them the reads a save makes to re-check something or to read itself back. R42 and P15 now say that a writer refused once any part of its line is written reports its save as not recorded, never as nothing written, the part staying past the recorded entries as a torn entry (D158, after verify-76; 9c265a3). R4 and R54 now say that the table loaded as v1 is judged by its bytes before anything else in it is checked, with whatever else keeps it from loading named after the changed-table refusal, and R4 that a value the format does not allow in a rule's field is named by rule, never raised as a type error (D158, after verify-77; 7cb2e71). No other requirement changes. The owner built these before this revision, and none is reviewed yet: T12's 9c265a3 and 51c8b68, and T03's 7cb2e71. At 51c8b68 the reads a save makes only to check something still turn the file system's error into an answer that names nothing, and before the line into a conflict that asks the person to reload; R42 as revision 19 writes it rules that out, so revision 19 asks the owner whether R42's naming reaches those reads (Options considered). At 7cb2e71 one validator path still raises a type error, for a bank-code rule whose pattern is a list (Context, T03's twenty-fifth review). The design takes in verify-76 and verify-77 and D154 to D158. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, C5, the traceability table, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 20, 23 and 24.
11:Revision 18, 2026-11-20, after D153 (changes requested under the owner's standing approval of 2026-11-19, after T12's twenty-fifth review, verify-74, and T03's twenty-fourth, verify-75). What changed from revision 17, keeping every id. D149 answered revision 17's question with its default: the take-back re-checks both lock files before its rename, as every head rename does, and a take-back that re-check stops answers not confirmed; R42 has said so since revision 17, and 7c8fba2 builds it. R42 now also says that when the file system fails a step a save takes after its check, the save answers, naming the error, which never reaches the caller as itself: not recorded before the rename that publishes its head record, and not confirmed after it, when the head record cannot be made durable (D153, after verify-74). R43 now says that a read taken again goes through the gate and the case lock, so a busy-gate read whose case lock is removed during it looks again, never reading as a reader that finds no gate (D153, after verify-74). R4 now says that only the frozen v1 table, known by the hash pinned in code, is spared the check that no rule carries an exception, and that every other table, whether or not it calls itself frozen, is checked rule by rule and refused naming the rule (D153, after verify-75). The term not confirmed widens to its second cause. No other requirement changes, and P15, which asks less than R42 and R43 here, does not. The owner built these before this revision, and neither commit is reviewed yet: T12's 7c8fba2 and T03's 9042fb4. 7c8fba2 maps the file system's errors where the head record is published; at a save's other steps they still reach the caller as themselves, which R42 as revision 18 writes it rules out, so revision 18 asks the owner how far R42's clause should reach (Options considered). The design takes in verify-74 and verify-75 and D149 to D153. The changes are in this header, Context, Options considered, C1's table validation, C3, C5, the traceability table, Out of scope, Consequences, the perspective check, the case log contract, the workspace API, Failure modes, Migration, Rollout, Observability and Consumer trace items 17, 20 and 23 and a new item 24.
98:- T12 builds the hash-chained case log and its single-writer lock for P15; R42 and R43 complete later, when sign-off (T27) and the other writers record into it. Detection passed from the start. Every failed round was about concurrency, and each found a new corner: a lock error read as another writer, which excused a torn entry (D50); a failed unlock left the lock held (D51); a forked child entered, or released, its parent's lock (D56, D57, D59); then, in the fifth review (verify-34), concurrent forks raced inside the fork hooks themselves, and a second handle opened through a symlinked folder took its own thread's lock for another writer's and reported a torn log as intact.
192:- A hard link needs a file system that has them. NTFS, APFS and the usual Linux file systems do; FAT and exFAT, common on USB sticks, do not, and there a first append fails with the file system's error and writes nothing (Failure modes).
204:- D138 approved revision 15, and verify-70, T12's twenty-third review, judged the owner's cd298cc against it and failed P15 on two majors. First, a reader that held the gate shared through a steady read never checked that the gate was still the case's. With only the gate file replaced during the read, the writer that held the case let go, a second writer took the same case lock through the new gate, and both of the reader's looks at the case lock found it held. So the reader reported the recorded entries and the rest as being saved, though no one writer held the case through its read (src/cma/case.py:488 there). Second, a read-back that failed after the head record was published, with a one-shot read error, told the save it was not recorded while the head record still listed its entry. A fresh check then read the log intact with that entry, and the next holder had no torn entry to drop (:974 there). Four minors, three of them recurring: the producer claimed exit 0 with 'appending' for a held torn tail, where the contract test expects exit 4 and 'being saved'; the stress test does not force the interleaving claimed for it; owner-only access was claimed for `cma case check`, which relies on the case root's permissions (R31) and checks no owner; and the claim's suite totals and diff-stat describe an earlier patch.
230:- verify-72, T12's twenty-fourth review, judged the owner's f5436bb against revision 16 and failed P15 on three majors. First, the read revision 16 named as open: a check that found the gate busy and held the case lock shared returned its read without comparing that lock file with its path. With the case lock replaced during the read and entry 2 appended under the new one, the check reported entry 2 missing (src/cma/case.py:530 there). Second, a reader that held the case returned its read without comparing the lock files it took. With the case lock replaced during the read and entry 2 appended through another handle under the new one, the holder reported entry 2 missing from its stale read (:459 there). Third, the take-back reused the head record's publication, whose lock re-check refused it. With the gate replaced just after a save's head record was published and a one-shot read error in the read-back, the save said it was not recorded, and a fresh check read the log intact with its entry, which no repair could drop (:1006 there). Three minors recur: the producer claimed exit 0 with 'appending' for a held torn tail, where the contract test expects exit 4 and 'being saved'; the stress test does not force the interleaving claimed for it; and the claim's suite totals and diff-stat describe an earlier patch.
252:- D150 approved revision 17. D153: write D149 into R42; say in R42 that a failure after the head record's rename, which cannot be made durable, answers not confirmed, one before it not recorded, and that no raw error reaches the caller; say in R43 that a busy-gate read whose case lock is removed during it looks again; and say in R4 that only the pinned frozen table, v1, skips the v2 table checks, a table calling itself frozen being still checked rule by rule, naming the rule. Keep everything else in revision 17. Revision 18 writes all of it. It reads 'no raw error' for every step of a save after its check, as D153's words read, and asks the owner whether that reach is meant, since 7c8fba2 maps the errors only where the head record is published. It reads 'a table calling itself frozen' for every table but v1, as 9042fb4's test says (the section after next).
255:- verify-74, T12's twenty-fifth review, judged the owner's 3076547 against revision 17 and failed P15 on three majors. First, the take-back checked only that the head record was still the save's own, so the schedule revision 17 named played out: a repair told recorded had its head record overwritten by the take-back, and a later confirmed drop left an intact log without it (src/cma/case.py:1041 there; the test at tests/test_case_log.py:2401 expected the take-back to go through a replaced lock file). Second, a check that found the gate busy and held the case lock shared compared that lock file with its path after its read; with the file removed, not replaced, the comparison raised the error a missing file raises, which the reader took for a case with no gate, so it trusted an unprotected steady read that could report a live append as a torn entry (:563 there). Third, a failed folder sync after a head record's rename reached the caller as the operating system's error, though the head record already listed the save (:986 there). Two minors recur: the producer claimed exit 0 with 'appending' for a held torn tail, where the contract test expects exit 4 and 'being saved', and the stress test does not force the interleaving claimed for it.
256:- The owner built 7c8fba2, not yet reviewed; D151, the unblock, is pending. The take-back re-checks both lock files and the head record before its rename, through the same re-checked publication as every head record, and answers not confirmed when it is not made (_publish, src/cma/case.py:1049-1061). The busy-gate read takes a case lock missing at its comparison as removed during the read and looks again (:553-562). An error from the file system while the head record is published answers not recorded when the head record still lists what the save's check read, and not confirmed otherwise, the folder sync after the rename having failed (:1031-1041). Four tests mark it: a failed read-back with the lock replaced, answering not confirmed (tests/test_case_log.py:2401, in place of 3076547's test that the take-back goes through), a head record that cannot be made durable, answering not confirmed (:2421), a busy-gate read whose case lock vanishes, looking again (:2440, named p15), and a take-back that fails, answering not confirmed (:2467).
257:- The first finding is the schedule revision 17 kept the re-check for; D149 answers it. The second reads R43's 'taken again' at its finest grain: a read taken again goes through the locks, and D153 writes that into R43. The third is R42's 'otherwise ... not recorded or ... not confirmed': the operating system's error is neither, and D153 writes the two answers into R42.
259:  - An error from the file system at a save's other steps still reaches the caller as itself: the first empty head record (_create_empty_head, :945-963), the log's open, the line's write and sync and the folder sync after a new log (:1001-1015), and a repair's read, staging file, log rename and folder sync (_replace_torn, :893-921). Revision 18's R42 rules it out at every step after the save's check, and revision 18 asks the owner whether that reach is meant (Options considered).
263:- The lesson is revision 13's, at the error path: what a writer tells its caller has to cover every way a save can end, an error from the file system included.
274:- D155 approved revision 18. D158: write D154 into R42, that every step of a save after its check reports an operating-system error as the save not recorded, naming the error, except where the head record was published (not confirmed); write into R42 and P15 that a lock lost after part of a line is written reports the save as not recorded, never nothing written, the part staying as a torn entry past the recorded entries (9c265a3); and write into R4 and R54 that a changed frozen v1 is refused as a changed frozen table before anything else is checked, with what else it breaks named after that, and that a malformed pattern value is named by rule, never raised as a type error (7cb2e71). Keep everything else in revision 18. Revision 19 writes all of it.
275:- Three readings. 'Except where the head record was published' is read as a head record that still lists the save's entry when the save answers: a save whose read-back fails and whose take-back is made has taken its head record back, and R42 has called it not recorded since revision 16. 'A malformed pattern value' is read for every rule field, as C1 has said for an id since revision 13. And 'every step' is read to take in the reads a save makes only to check, a re-check of a lock file, the log or the head record, and the read-back, since R42 lists reading among a save's steps. At 51c8b68 those reads still name no error, and revision 19 asks the owner whether that reach is meant (Options considered).
279:- The owner built 9c265a3 and then 51c8b68, neither yet reviewed; D156, the unblock, is pending. In 9c265a3 a refusal raised while the line is being written answers not recorded, saying that part of its entry was written, whenever the log is longer than the save's check found it (src/cma/case.py:1024-1035 at 51c8b68); a refusal before any byte of the line still says nothing was written. In 51c8b68 every operating-system error raised from an append's or a repair's steps after its check reaches the caller as the save not recorded, naming the error (_os_errors_as_not_recorded, :842-852, around _write at :880 and _replace_torn at :897), while _publish's own answer, not recorded or not confirmed, stands where the head record is published (:1056-1066). Tests mark both: a lock lost after part of the line (tests/test_case_log.py:2488), an operating-system error at the line, the first empty head record and a repair's rename (:2513), and the three tests that crashed a save with an OSError, now expecting not recorded (:52, :717 and :1715). The two new tests are marked invariants but not named p15.
282:  - The reads a save makes only to check something turn the file system's error into a value, so the answer names no error and sometimes gives the wrong cause. The append's comparison of the log's bytes with its check (:1018) and a repair's re-check before its log rename (:921) read an unreadable log as changed and refuse with a conflict that asks to reload. The head record's re-check before its rename (:995) says the head file changed. The read-back (:1067) says the log changed, and a take-back that fails (:1075) names no error. A lock file that cannot be examined is reported as removed or replaced (:795-799). R42 as revision 19 writes it asks each of these to name the error, and the conflicts to answer not recorded; revision 19 asks the owner (Options considered).
299:- D159 answered revision 19's question with its default: the reads a save makes only to check something name an operating-system error, and a re-check that cannot read answers not recorded, never a conflict. The owner built it in 1e35352 (next section).
300:- D160 approved revision 19. D163 asks for three things, and keeps everything else in revision 19. First, write D159 into R42: the reads a save makes to check something (the log comparison, the re-checks of the head record and the log, the read-back, the lock-file check) name an operating-system error, and a re-check that cannot read answers not recorded (or not confirmed after its head record was published), never a conflict to reload or a lock removed or replaced. Second, write into R42 and P15 that once any of the line is on disk, no part of a save's report says nothing was written (de6a56e). Third, write into R4 that a malformed pattern on a bank-code rule is named by its rule, and that any failure of a changed v1 is named as a changed frozen table first (5139e22). Revision 20 writes all of it.
305:- The owner built de6a56e and then 1e35352, neither yet reviewed; D161, the unblock, is pending. In de6a56e a refusal at the head rename is reported in the save's own words: its entry was written and the save was not recorded (_publish, src/cma/case.py:1059-1064 at 1e35352). In 1e35352 the reads a save makes only to check raise the operating system's error instead of turning it into a value:
308:  - the read-back (:1076-1085), which names the error as the read-back's failure;
310:  _os_errors_as_not_recorded (:848-858) then reports the save as not recorded, naming the error, and _publish reports it as not recorded or not confirmed. Two tests mark it. The first is a lock lost after the whole line, whose report never says nothing was written (tests/test_case_log.py:2532). The second injects a permission error at the log comparison, the head record's re-check, the read-back, the lock files' examination and a repair's re-check; the report names it and never says the case changed or a lock file was removed or replaced (:2551). Both are marked invariants but not named p15.
314:  - When the head record's publication fails before its rename, _publish tells not recorded from not confirmed by reading the head record back without raising (:1065-1075, the read at :1069). The head record may not be readable, as when the re-check before the rename failed for that very reason, or it may read as another writer's under interference. Either way the save answers not confirmed and says the head record could not be made durable, though no rename was made; R42 asks for not recorded there. The D159 test injects its error into the re-check alone, so the read at :1069 succeeds and the test sees not recorded.
325:  - The refusal for a failure no check foresees, on any table but a changed v1, names no rule (:691). No path to it is known, since every type error verify-67 to verify-79 found now has a check of its own. If one remains, though, the table is refused without the rule and field R4 asks for.
332:- Three readings. 'Its causes included' is read as the words of any refusal or error a report carries or is raised from, as 7fb3a23's test reads the whole exception chain (tests/test_case_log.py:2650). 'One answer per report' is read for every report a save's writer gets, so a not-recorded report says nothing of recorded or not confirmed either; no not-recorded report at 7fb3a23 does. And 'a changed v1 names its forbidden exceptions' is read as one more thing named after the changed-table refusal. Beyond the exception check, a changed v1 is judged as the kind of table it says it is, as 5f5bb2e builds. A copy that still calls itself frozen is not held to the stages, the category order and the other checks only the maintained lists need, which v1's own rules lack; a copy set to frozen: false is (tests/test_rules.py:217). R4 says so, and its 'every other table' now reads 'every table the system does not load as v1', since a changed v1 bears v1's id. v1 carries no exception and only the bank's code as an anchor (src/cma/rules/v1.yaml:13), so the check names only what a change adds.
336:- The owner built 26bc51e and then 7fb3a23, neither yet reviewed; the unblock is pending. In 26bc51e the lock check's refusal ends 'the step was refused', not 'nothing was written' (_require_lock_in_place, src/cma/case.py:807-811 at 7fb3a23), so no refusal a save's report is raised from says nothing was written. _write_head notes when its rename is done (:1009), and _publish answers not recorded unless that rename was made (:1076), never reading the head record back to decide. In 7fb3a23 a take-back that is not made is named in the save's own words: a head record no longer the save's own, a case lock file removed or replaced, or the operating-system error, named. Its refusal is neither quoted nor chained (:1106-1121), and every not-confirmed report now says that whether the save is in the log is not confirmed (:1080, :1120). Three tests mark it: no part of the report or its causes says nothing was written, after the whole line and with the take-back refused (tests/test_case_log.py:2601); a head record that cannot be read before its rename answers not recorded (:2631); and a not-confirmed report, with the case lock replaced or the head record another writer's, says nothing in its chain of recorded, not recorded or nothing written (:2650). All three are marked invariants but not named p15.
366:  - *A report's words after its line* (reviews 29 to 31). A nested save's refusal, which said nothing was written, became the report of a save whose line was in the log (verify-82; 90c6094). A repair interrupted after its log landed reported only the nested refusal, and a refusal during the read-back skipped the take-back (verify-84; c466717). An interruption just after a rename, or any exception during a take-back, escaped as itself or read a cleared flag (verify-86; 42e687d). Since 42e687d, whatever stops a save once its line has started to reach the log is reported as the save's own answer (_os_errors_as_not_recorded, src/cma/case.py:904-950 at 523c5df), as R42 asks.
404:Its reader, CaseLog.verify (:444), holds the gate shared for each attempt and then tries the case lock shared. If the case lock is free, or there is no case lock file behind the gate, it reads once and judges the whole log. If the case lock is held, it judges a steady read and tries the case lock again: it judges the whole log if the writer let go, and otherwise reports the recorded entries and the rest as being saved. A check that finds the gate busy, because a writer is passing it or paused there, tries the case lock directly and reads holding it if it is free (D98). If the case is held too, a steady read names a fault at or before the recorded entries, but only within a head record it read (D105, D116), and otherwise the check tries again. With no gate file, a steady read stands unless a gate appears meanwhile. When the retries run out, it reports being saved and claims nothing only if some attempt saw the case lock held, and otherwise that the log could not be checked (D120). A lock that cannot be taken or tested blames no other window (_untested, :572). `cma case check` (src/cma/cli.py) loads it and exits 0, 3 or 4 as D75 decided, 4 for both answers that check nothing. At f5436bb (the owner's review-23 fix, not yet reviewed) the lines from :545 move by 23 and those from :987 by 33. After each read the reader compares each lock file it holds with what its path names now, and takes the read again if one was removed or replaced (:476-480, used at :489, :498, :505 and :509); the read a busy-gate check takes holding the case lock alone is not yet compared (:527-533). A save whose read-back fails takes back the head record it published, when it is still its own, by publishing the head record its check read through the same re-checked rename (_publish, :985-1011, the take-back at :999-1011). At 3076547 (the owner's review-24 fix, 0d339cc, and D143's answer, neither yet reviewed), a holder's read counts only while the case lock it holds and the gate it passed are still the case's, and otherwise goes on as any other reader's (verify, :451-482). The read a busy-gate check takes holding the case lock alone compares that lock file with its path (:553-558). _publish (:1017-1049) takes back its head record checking only that the head record is still its own, whatever the lock files (:1032-1042), and raises SaveNotConfirmed (:107-110) when the take-back is not made (:1043-1047). At 7c8fba2 (the owner's review-25 fix, not yet reviewed) the busy-gate read takes its case lock missing at the comparison after its read as removed and looks again (:553-562). _publish (:1019-1063) maps an error from the file system in publishing the head record to not recorded or not confirmed (:1031-1041), and re-checks both lock files and the head record before the take-back's rename, answering not confirmed when the take-back is not made (:1049-1061). At 51c8b68 (the owner's review-26 fix, 9c265a3, and D154's mapping, neither yet reviewed) a refusal raised while the line is being written answers not recorded, saying part of its entry was written, whenever the log is longer than the save's check found it (:1024-1035); every operating-system error raised from an append's or a repair's steps after its check reaches the caller as the save not recorded, naming the error (_os_errors_as_not_recorded, :842-852, around _write at :880 and _replace_torn at :897); and _publish's own answer, not recorded or not confirmed, stands where the head record is published (:1056-1066). The reads a save makes only to check, the append's byte comparison (:1018), a repair's re-check (:921), the head record's re-check (:995), the read-back (:1067), the take-back (:1075) and the lock files' examination (:795-799), still turn the error into a value. At 1e35352 (the owner's review-27 fix, de6a56e, and D159's reads, neither yet reviewed) a refusal at the head rename after the line is reported in the save's own words: its entry was written and the save was not recorded (_publish, :1059-1064). The reads a save makes only to check raise the operating system's error instead of turning it into a value: the log's digest (_log_digest, :957-963, at :927 and :1024), the head record's re-checks (_read_head with raise_os, :247-259, at :927 and :1001), the read-back (:1076-1085), and the examination of both lock files, where only a missing file reads as removed or replaced (_require_lock_in_place, :793-808). _os_errors_as_not_recorded (:848-858) and _publish (:1050-1105) name the error. The take-back still quotes its refusal in its not-confirmed report (:1100-1104). At 7fb3a23 (the owner's review-28 fix, 26bc51e, and D164's wording, neither yet reviewed) the lock check's refusal ends 'the step was refused' (_require_lock_in_place, :794-811). _write_head notes when its rename is done (:1009), and _publish (:1055-1122) answers not recorded unless that rename was made (:1071-1081). It names a take-back that is not made in the save's own words, neither quoting nor chaining its refusal (:1106-1121). At 523c5df (the owner's review-39 fix, not yet reviewed) a hold's check of its lock files (_Hold.in_place, :438-453) looks at both paths, then at the link counts of both files held, then at both paths again. It serves both the holder's read (verify, :512-523) and a writer's re-check before each step (_require_lock_in_place, :839-859). Any other reader looks at each file it holds and then the first again (:540-549). One save at a time is a single step (_one_save, :887-902). _os_errors_as_not_recorded (:904-950) reports whatever stops a save once its line has started to reach the log as the save's own answer. Every signal whose handler is Python code is held off from publishing the head record to the take-back (_interrupts_deferred, :1252-1291). The integration branch (463ceee) has no case log and no profile names yet.
427:**Option C: spreadsheet as the interface.** Export lines and figures to a workbook, let the underwriter edit a category column, and re-import. It is familiar: they add things up in a spreadsheet today. *It lost because* a figure loses its link to its evidence as soon as a cell is edited or copied. The tool also cannot tell a category correction from an edited amount, and re-importing a hand-edited workbook is the error-prone step the bet is meant to remove. A monthly-summary CSV stays as an export.
465:**Sub-decision: what a changed v1 is refused for first (revision 19, after verify-77; D158, in R4 and R54 since revision 19).** Since revision 18 only the frozen v1 table, known by the hash pinned in code, is spared the table checks. At 9042fb4 the loader parsed and checked a table loaded as v1 before it compared its bytes with the pin, so a copy of v1 that kept id: v1 and frozen: true with its rules list emptied was refused for having no rules, never as changed (verify-77). (i) Compare the bytes first; refuse any other bytes as a changed frozen table, naming the table, whatever the table says of itself, and name after that whatever else keeps it from loading (7cb2e71). (ii) Check the table first, as 9042fb4 did: a changed v1 that is also malformed is refused for the malformation, and the message that matters, reinstall the package, is lost. (iii) Compare the bytes only, and name nothing else: a person mending a hand-edited copy learns one fault at a time. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D158), which R4 and R54 say since revision 19. What (i) costs: a changed v1 is still parsed, to name what else it breaks; none of its rules is used. The same revision asks the validator to survive every value the format can hold: a value the format does not allow in a rule's field is named by rule, beside the table's other faults, never raised as a type error (R4 since revision 19). Revision 21 asks the same of a pattern that compiles alone but cannot be used in a form the matcher builds from it: every rule's matchers are built while the table is checked, so such a pattern is named beside the table's other faults, never found once they are mended (D168, after verify-81; 5f5bb2e).
501:**Sub-decision: how the first empty head record is published (revision 15, after verify-68).** The first append writes an empty head record before its line (revision 9, D87), so that a missing head record beside any entry is damage. 1d16873 published it like every head record, by renaming a staged file into place. verify-68 paused a first append just before that rename. With the case lock file replaced, B took the new lock, appended entry 1 and was told it was recorded. A's rename then put an empty head record over B's, B's entry became a torn write past the recorded entries, and a confirmed repair left a log that read intact without a save told recorded, which R42 rules out since revision 13. (i) Create the empty head record only where none exists: link the staged file into place, which fails if a head record is there, and stop with a conflict (cd298cc). (ii) Rename as before, after re-checking that no head record exists: the check and the rename are two steps, and verify-68's schedule fits between them. (iii) Create the head record exclusively and write it in place: a reader could find the file created but still empty, which reads as a damaged head record, not an empty one. *Chose (i)*, which the owner built in cd298cc (D134). R42's text already covers it, so no requirement changes. It closes the window rather than narrowing it, because the link itself refuses. What (i) costs: a file system with hard links. NTFS, APFS and the usual Linux file systems have them; FAT and exFAT, common on USB sticks, do not, and there the first append fails with the file system's error and writes nothing. The README and the trial sheet name the file systems to use.
505:**Sub-decision: a save whose read-back fails (revision 16, after verify-70; D141, in R42 since revision 16).** A save is told recorded only after reading back an intact log that holds its entry (D125). verify-70 failed that read-back with a one-shot read error: the save was told it was not recorded while its head record still listed its entry, so a fresh check read the log intact with the entry, and the next holder had no torn entry to drop, where P15 says a save refused after its line leaves its entry past the recorded entries. (i) Leave the head record as published (cd298cc). (ii) Take back the head record the save published, when it is still the save's own, by putting back the head record its check read, through a head rename re-checked as every head rename is (f5436bb). (iii) Read back again until a read succeeds. *Chose (ii)*, the owner's answer (D141), which R42 says since revision 16. (i) lost on verify-70. (iii) lost because a lasting error would hold the save, and a read-back that finds the entry gone, after a repair under interference, would still leave the save's head record listing it. A head record another writer has published since is left alone, since putting back the old one would remove that writer's save. What (ii) costs: one more rename when a read-back fails, and a take-back that can itself fail, which the next two sub-decisions take up.
507:**Sub-decision: a take-back that cannot be made (revision 16; D143, in R42 and P15 since revision 17).** The take-back is a head rename, so it re-checks both lock files first (R42), and it can fail: the head record cannot be read or renamed, or a lock file was replaced. At f5436bb the save then says it was not recorded and that its head record could not be taken back, and asks for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved (src/cma/case.py:1004-1011). Its entry stays among the recorded entries, so the next check reads the log intact with it. (i) Report the save as not confirmed, neither recorded nor not recorded, and ask for the case log to be checked, which shows whether the entry is there; R42 and P15 name that third answer in the next revision. (ii) Keep 'not recorded', and write into R42 and P15 that such a save's entry can stay among the recorded entries. (iii) Retry the take-back until it is made or the head record is no longer the save's own. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D143), which R42 and P15 say since revision 17 (D148) and 3076547 builds as SaveNotConfirmed. (ii) lost because the tool would tell a person a save was not recorded while the log holds it, and a person who saves again records the change twice. (iii) lost because a lasting error, a full disk or a lost mount, would hold the window. What (i) costs: a third answer for a save, in R42 and P15, the workspace message and T12's exception; it happens only when a read-back and its take-back both fail.
509:**Sub-decision: what a take-back checks before its rename (revision 17, after verify-72; D148 asked for the head record alone; D149, in R42 since revision 17).** The take-back is a head rename (revision 16). verify-72 replaced the gate after a save published its head record and failed the read-back with a one-shot read error. f5436bb's take-back re-checked the lock files and refused, and the save said it was not recorded while its head record listed its entry. (i) Re-check both lock files and the head record before the take-back's rename, as before every head rename, and answer not confirmed when either check stops it (D143); verify-72's schedule then ends not confirmed. (ii) Check only that the head record is still the save's own, whatever the lock files, as 0d339cc builds and D148 asks R42 to say. (iii) Make no take-back, and answer not confirmed whenever a read-back fails. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D149), which R42 has said since revision 17 and 7c8fba2 builds; verify-74 then failed 3076547, which checked the head record alone, on (ii)'s schedule. (ii) loses to the schedule in Context (T12's twenty-fourth review). A repair that checked before the save's head record landed publishes its own head record after the take-back's check and is told recorded; the take-back then lands over it, and a later confirmed drop leaves an intact log without that repair's save. R42 rules that out, so (ii) would need a second exception in R42's guarantee, beside D124's. Under (i) the repair holds the case only through a replaced case lock, which the take-back's re-check finds. A writer that takes the case after that re-check checked a head record that lists the save's entry, so its own entry follows that entry. A take-back that then lands over its head record leaves two or more entries past the recorded ones, and the log reads broken, never intact. (iii) lost because a read error alone, with no interference, would leave the save not confirmed where a take-back makes it plainly not recorded. What (i) costs: under interference, a failed read-back after a lock file was replaced answers not confirmed, and the person checks the case log before saving again. 7c8fba2 turns the lock re-check back on for the take-back, and the test at tests/test_case_log.py:2401 now expects not confirmed.
511:**Sub-decision: a writer that takes the case through a gate replaced before the read (revision 16; D144, in R43 and P15 since revision 17).** A writer passes the gate, checks that the gate is still the case's, and then takes the case lock (src/cma/case.py:704-714 at f5436bb). Paused between the two, it can take the case lock after the gate file is replaced, through a gate no reader holds. Each later step it tries refuses, since the gate it passed is gone (R42), but the case shows as held. A reader that holds the new gate and finds the case held by the writer before it may then judge a read across which that writer let go, leaving an incomplete entry, and report being saved; its own lock files stay the case's, so revision 16's comparison passes. (i) State in R43 and P15 that, beyond a read across a lock file the reader holds, lock-file interference is outside what a reader can know, and that a check after it stops gives the true answer. (ii) Also have a writer re-check, once it holds the case lock, that the gate it passed is still the case's, and let the case go if not: this narrows the moment without closing it, since a reader can look while the writer holds the lock. (iii) Leave R43 and P15 as revision 16 writes them. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D144), which R43 and P15 say since revision 17 (D148). (iii) lost because R43 and P15 say when a reader may report being saved without saying that lock-file interference bounds it, and T12's reviewer judges P15 by its own text. (ii) is not chosen alone because it leaves the same statement to make. What (i) costs: one sentence in R43 and P15; no build change.
513:**Sub-decision: what a save answers when the file system fails one of its steps (revision 18, after verify-74; D153, in R42 since revision 18; its reach D154, in R42 since revision 19, D158; the reads a save makes only to check, D159, in R42 since revision 20, D163; the answer known from the rename, D168, in R42 since revision 21).** After its check a save reads, writes, syncs, links and renames files, and the file system can fail any of these: a full disk, an I/O error, a file system without hard links. verify-74 failed the folder sync just after a head record's rename. The head record already listed the save, and the caller got the operating system's error, which is none of R42's answers. (i) Answer by where the failure falls: before the rename that publishes the save's head record, not recorded, since the head record does not list the entry; after it, when the head record cannot be made durable, not confirmed, since the head record lists the entry but a power cut could still undo the rename. Either answer names the error, and the error never reaches the caller as itself. (ii) Take back the head record when its folder cannot be synced, as after a failed read-back: the take-back's rename needs the same sync to last, so it cannot make the answer surer. (iii) Let the operating system's error through, and document that the caller checks the case log. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D153), which R42 says since revision 18; 7c8fba2 builds it where the head record is published (_publish, src/cma/case.py:1031-1041), telling the two answers apart by whether the head record still lists what the save's check read. (iii) lost because a caller that hears an error it cannot place may save again over a save the log holds, and the workspace would have to turn each error into a message on its own. (ii) lost for the reason given. What (i) costs: after a failed sync the person checks the case log before saving again, and a check made before a power cut can show the entry recorded where one made after it shows a torn write; both are rare, and the check gives the log's answer at that moment. *Its reach* (D154; R42 since revision 19, D158): every step a save takes after its check, the owner's answer to revision 18's question. 51c8b68 maps the steps that change a file, the first empty head record, the log's open, the line's write and sync, the folder sync after a new log and a repair's read, staging file, log rename and folder sync, to not recorded, naming the error. Revision 19 writes the answer as one rule: not recorded, or not confirmed when the save's head record was published and not taken back. That reads D158's 'except where the head record was published' as a head record that still lists the save's entry when the save answers, since a save whose take-back is made is not recorded (R42 since revision 16). Taking the case comes before a save's check, so a lock that cannot be taken is as R43 says: the append fails with the lock's error and writes nothing. *The reads a save makes only to check (asked in revision 19; D159, read by read in R42 since revision 20, D163):* a save also reads in order to check: it compares the log's bytes and the head record with its check before its line and its renames, examines both lock files before each step, and reads itself back. At 51c8b68 each of those reads turns the file system's error into a value: an unreadable log reads as changed, an unreadable head record as changed, a lock file that cannot be examined as removed or replaced. The answer then names no error, and before the line it is a conflict that asks the person to reload (Context, T12's twenty-sixth review). (a) Count them among R42's steps: every answer names the error, and a re-check that cannot read what it checks answers not recorded, never a conflict; R42 says so since revision 19 (design default). (b) Keep R42's naming to the steps that change a file, and say in R42 that a re-check or a read-back that cannot read what it reads answers as one that finds a change: a conflict before the line, not recorded after it, and the take-back rules after the head record's rename, naming no error. *Chose (a)*, the owner's answer (D159), which R42 names read by read since revision 20 (D163) and 1e35352 builds, because D158 says every step, R42 lists reading among a save's steps, and a person told to reload a case whose log cannot be read reloads into the same error. What (a) costs: T12 carries the error out of six reads before its review. (b) costs no build change and leaves a disk error answered as somebody else's save. *Known from the rename* (revision 21, after verify-80; D168): (i) tells the two answers apart by where the failure falls, before or after the head record's rename. 7c8fba2 decided it by reading the head record back, and when that read failed too, as when the re-check before the rename failed for that very reason, it answered not confirmed though no rename was made (verify-80). Since 26bc51e the save notes when its rename is done and answers from that, never from a head record it may not be able to read, as R42 says since revision 21.
515:**Sub-decision: what a save refused partway through its line answers (revision 19, after verify-76; D158, in R42 and P15 since revision 19).** A writer re-checks both lock files before every write call of its line (D116), so a short write continued in a second call can be refused with part of the line already on disk. At 7c8fba2 that refusal said nothing was written, while the next check named the part as an incomplete entry (src/cma/case.py:1010 there). (i) Not recorded, never nothing written: the part stays past the recorded entries as a torn entry, as a crash leaves it, for the next holder's repair (9c265a3, which tells the two apart by whether the log is longer than the save's check found it). (ii) Cut the log back to the length the check found, then answer nothing written: that changes a file a reader reads after the writer lost its lock, which R42 refuses. (iii) Keep the message, and let the next check name the torn entry: the person is told nothing was written about a case that now needs a repair before anything else is saved. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D158), which R42 and P15 say since revision 19. What (i) costs: nothing beyond the message; the torn entry is what a crash at that moment leaves, and the existing repair drops it. Revision 20 extends (i) to every part of a save's report (D163, after verify-78; R42 and P15). At 51c8b68 a save refused at its head rename, after the whole line, was told it was not recorded in words that ended with the lock check's own, 'nothing was written'; de6a56e words that refusal for the save. A repair's line is in the log once its repaired log is renamed over the log. A repair refused before that rename leaves the case's files as they were, and 'nothing was written' is true of it. Revision 21 carries (i) into the report's causes: the words of any refusal or error a report is raised from are part of it (D168, after verify-80). 26bc51e words the lock check's own refusal as the step refused, so no cause a save carries says nothing was written; a save refused before any part of its line is in the log should still be told it was not recorded (Context, T12's twenty-eighth review).
517:**Sub-decision: what a not-confirmed report may quote (revision 20, after the owner's 1e35352; D164, in R42 since revision 21, D168).** A save whose read-back fails and whose take-back is refused is not confirmed (D143). At 1e35352 its report says so, and quotes in brackets the refusal that stopped the take-back (src/cma/case.py:1100-1104). Two refusals can stand there. A replaced case lock file gives the lock check's refusal, which ends 'nothing was written' (:807-808), though the save's line is in the log. R42 and P15 rule that out since revision 20, so T12 changes it before its review whichever option is chosen. A head record no longer the save's own gives the head check's refusal, which ends 'the save was not recorded' (:1002-1003), inside a report that R42 calls neither recorded nor not recorded. (i) One answer per report. The take-back's refusal is worded for the save, naming what stopped it: a lock file removed or replaced, a head record another writer published, or the error met. No part of a not-confirmed report says that the save was recorded, was not recorded or wrote nothing. R42 says so in the next revision (design default). (ii) A not-confirmed report may quote what stopped its take-back in that refusal's own words, and only 'nothing was written' is ruled out; R42 says so in the next revision. (iii) Name no cause, and say only that the save is not confirmed. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D164), which R42 says since revision 21 (D168) and 7fb3a23 builds. A person who reads 'the save was not recorded' inside a not-confirmed report may save again over a save the log holds, which is what D143 ended. T12's reviewer also reads R42's 'neither recorded nor not recorded' at its finest grain, as verify-78 read 'never as nothing written'. (ii) keeps that risk for the sake of a quotation. (iii) loses what the person should look for before saving again: a lock file a cleanup tool touched, or another window's save. What (i) costs: T12 words two refusals for the take-back, as de6a56e did for the head rename, and its test at tests/test_case_log.py:2401 asserts the words as well as the class. D168 adds the report's causes: 7fb3a23 neither quotes nor chains the take-back's refusal, and 26bc51e words the lock check's own refusal as the step refused, so no refusal or error a save's report carries says nothing was written (tests/test_case_log.py:2601, :2650).
519:**Sub-decision: a busy-gate read whose case lock is removed during it (revision 18, after verify-74; D153, in R43 since revision 18).** A check that finds the gate busy holds the case lock shared and reads, and its read counts only while that case lock is still the case's (R43 since revision 17). verify-74 removed the case lock file, rather than replacing it, between the read and the comparison. The comparison raised the error a missing file raises, which 3076547's reader took for the absence of a gate, so it trusted a steady read that no lock file kept writers out of, and a writer that created and took a new case lock meanwhile could have its live append reported torn. (i) A lock file missing at the comparison was removed during the read: the reader looks again through the gate and the case lock, as at its first attempt (7c8fba2). (ii) Treat the missing case lock as a case no writer holds and judge the read, as a reader behind the gate with no case lock file does: that reader holds the gate, which keeps writers out, and this one does not. (iii) Report the case as being saved: the reader cannot know that a save is under way. *Chose (i)*, the owner's answer (D153), which R43 says since revision 18 for every read taken again. What (i) costs: nothing beyond the attempts the retries already allow; with every attempt so, the check claims nothing, as R43 says.
564:  - *Table validation* (revision 6; revision 10, D94, D96, D97; revision 11, D110; revision 13, D115-D121; revision 14, D126; revision 15, D136; revision 16, D137; revision 21, D168; revision 22, D192): a table is refused at load, naming the rule, in nine cases. First, a pattern that does not compile in every form the matcher uses, including the form that finds matches at every position (a global inline flag such as (?i)INV[0-9]+ is one way to fail). Second, a v2 pattern that uses a back-reference or a conditional group, whose meaning would change when the matcher wraps it, or an atomic group or a possessive repeat, which no later end can reopen, so the longest match could not be found (D117). Third, a v2 pattern that can match no text, such as a lookahead alone, which leaves no span for the longer-phrase rule to set aside (D121). Fourth, since revision 11 (D110), a v2 pattern with a repeat whose body can match both a space and a letter or digit (.*, [A-Z ]+), whose match would reach across words and take in other lists' wording. Fifth, the bank's code: the bank-code flag anywhere but on the returned-payment rule whose whole pattern is one of the bank return codes listed in code, with no names, no exception and a direction that includes credits; and a v2 table in which a listed code has no rule that carries the flag, or carries the code's pattern without it (D96, D97, D115, D117, D119). Sixth, a v2 rule id that holds anything but letters, digits, _, . and -, or that is default, round_trip or conflict, the categoriser's own provenance (D119, D121); an id that is not a string is named like any other malformed field, never raised as an error (verify-67's and verify-71's minor and verify-77's major; R4 says so since revision 19, for every rule field; verify-79 found the last such path, the gathering of the codes the bank-code rules carry, for a bank-code rule whose pattern is a list, which 5139e22 closes at :534-535; since revision 20 R4 says a malformed value is named by its rule whatever else the rule carries, the bank-code flag included, D163). Seventh, since D126 (R4 since revision 14; 4adddf9), an exception, an unless or a requires, on any v2 rule, which could switch a rule off where another list's wording sits and so decide a collision before the ambiguity check sees it (verify-65, verify-67). Eighth, since D136 (R4 since revision 15; 78556a7), a v2 pattern with a lookahead or lookbehind, an exception written into the pattern, which tests wording beside its match without matching it (verify-69). The whole-word test the matcher adds is the matcher's own, and \b written in a pattern looks only at the character on each side, as whole words do, so neither is an exception. Ninth, since D137 (R4 since revision 16; 2c72469), a v2 pattern with an anchor (^, $, \A or \Z), which tests whether anything stands before or after its match in the line; only the bank's code listed in code keeps its ^, on the rule that carries the bank-code flag. So a table never loads and then fails on the first credit it matches, a table edit cannot give payer wording the bank's standing or switch the bank's code off, no pattern can hide another list's words by reaching over them, no rule can hide them behind an exception, in its keys or in its pattern, and a line's provenance always names one decision. Only the frozen v1 table is spared these checks (R4 since revision 18, D153, after verify-75; 9042fb4), and the seventh to ninth, the exception checks, only as its bytes were verified (R4 since revision 21, D168, after verify-81; 5f5bb2e). Only a digest of its bytes that matches the pin verifies them: a table checked with no digest, or with another, has its exceptions named whatever it says of itself (R4 since revision 22, D192, after verify-103; 4c017d4). The table the system loads as v1 is judged by its bytes before anything else in it is checked (R4 and R54 since revision 19, D158, after verify-77; 7cb2e71): with any bytes but the pinned ones it is refused as a changed frozen table, naming the table, whatever it says of itself, with whatever else keeps it from loading named after that and none of its rules used, whatever the change makes fail (R4 since revision 20, D163, after verify-79; 5139e22, which catches every failure of a changed v1, a type error included). A changed v1 names, after the changed-table refusal, each of its rules that carries an exception. Beyond that it is judged as the kind of table it says it is: a copy that still calls itself frozen is not held to the checks only the maintained lists need (stages, the category order, rule ids and the v2 pattern forms), which v1's own rules lack, and a copy set to frozen: false gets them all (R4 since revision 21). Every table not loaded as v1, whether or not it calls itself frozen, gets every check and is refused naming the rule. Every rule's matchers, in every form the matcher uses, are built while the table is checked, so a pattern that compiles alone but cannot be used is named with the table's other faults, whatever else its rule carries, never found once they are mended (R4 since revision 21, D168, after verify-81; 5f5bb2e; for a rule with another malformed field since 48c5b9c and edc3ff4, after verify-83 and verify-85). A key given more than once, in the table or in a rule, is a fault named with the others, and every value it was given is still checked as a key given once is. That means the rules of every rules list, and each value of a repeated field, compiled, built and given every check a pattern given once gets, whichever value comes first. R4 says so since revision 22 for compiling, building and exceptions (D192, after verify-97 to verify-103). At 4c017d4 only the last value is compiled and built, and the checks for a back-reference, no text, an atomic group or a possessive repeat and a repeat across words read none of the values (Context). A failure that no check foresees is still refused, but at 4c017d4 its refusal names no rule (src/cma/categorise.py:886-892).
594:    - *A save is told recorded only after it reads back*, once its head record is published, an intact log that holds its entry where that head record lists it. Otherwise it says the save was not recorded and that the case should be checked. A save also publishes its head record only if the head record still lists what its check read. If the read-back fails, the save takes back the head record it published, when that head record is still its own, by putting back the head record its check read (revision 16, D141, after verify-70; R42); the take-back is a head rename, re-checked as every head rename is (D149; R42 since revision 17; 7c8fba2). Its entry is then past the recorded entries, as a crash leaves it, and the next holder confirms dropping it. A head record another writer has published since is left alone. Suppose the take-back is not made, because the head record is no longer the save's own, a lock file was removed or replaced, or the head record cannot be read or renamed. The entry then stays where it is, and the save is reported as not confirmed, neither recorded nor not recorded, with a request to check the case log, which shows whether the entry is among the recorded entries (D143; R42 and P15 since revision 17). That report names what stopped the take-back in the save's own words: the head record no longer the save's own, a lock file removed or replaced, or the operating-system error, named. It never quotes the take-back's refusal, and no part of it, its causes included, says the save was recorded, was not recorded or that nothing was written (D164; R42 since revision 21, D168; 7fb3a23, which neither quotes nor chains the refusal, src/cma/case.py:1106-1121).
602:  - *A save the file system fails* (revision 18, D153, after verify-74; R42). After its check, a save reads, writes, syncs, links and renames files, and the file system can fail any of these: a full disk, an I/O error, a file system without hard links. The save then answers, naming the error, and the error itself never reaches its caller. Before the rename that publishes its head record, the save is not recorded: whatever part of its line was written stays past the recorded entries, as a crash leaves it, for the next holder to drop. After that rename the head record lists the save, and if the folder that holds it cannot then be synced, a power cut could still undo the rename, so the save is not confirmed and asks for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved; it makes no read-back or take-back then, since a take-back would need the same sync to last. A read-back whose read fails is a failed read-back, taken back as above, and a failure in a take-back answers not confirmed. Taking the case comes before the save's check, so a lock that cannot be taken is as R43 says: the append fails with the lock's error and writes nothing. Since revision 19 R42 states this as one rule (D158): every operating-system error a save meets after its check, in a step or in a read it makes to re-check or read itself back, answers not recorded, naming the error, unless the save's head record was published and not taken back, when it answers not confirmed. 7c8fba2 builds it where the head record is published, telling the two answers apart by whether the head record still lists what the save's check read (_publish, src/cma/case.py:1056-1066 at 51c8b68), and 51c8b68 at every other step that changes a file (_os_errors_as_not_recorded, :842-852). Since revision 20 R42 names the reads a save makes only to check: the comparison of the log with its check, the re-checks of the head record, the read-back and the examination of both lock files. One that cannot be made is reported as its error, never as a conflict to reload or a lock file removed or replaced, and a lock file missing when the writer examines it was removed (D159, D163). 1e35352 builds it (Context, T12's twenty-seventh review). Since revision 21 R42 says that whether the head record's rename was made is known from the rename, never inferred from reading the head record back (D168, after verify-80): a head record that cannot be read in the re-check before its rename stops the rename, and the save answers not recorded. 26bc51e builds it: the save notes when its rename is done and answers from that (src/cma/case.py:1009, :1076 at 7fb3a23). At 1e35352 such a save answered not confirmed.
605:- **C5 Review workspace** (new src/cma/review/). A loopback server and one bundled page with no build step. The server is one process that serves requests on threads and never forks workers, so every window's writes stay inside the case log's scope (R42). It renders what C2 and policy.evaluate computed and never computes money. The override form offers only categories valid for the line's direction. While show_policy_verdict is on, a policy-check panel shows the outcome and each rule's result on the current figures, with the illustrative label in the panel. The judgement queue comes first (R15). For an ambiguous credit it shows each candidate and the bucket it would give: a table rule by its list, a payment-method candidate as giving no bucket, and a profile candidate as a name from the case profile with the bucket its kind gives, so the underwriter's override starts from the candidates. The drill-down shows each line's rule as recorded, which for an ambiguous credit is the conflict form (R16). While another window is saving, the case view says the case is being saved; it shows a broken-log warning only for something wrong at or before the recorded entries, or for an incomplete entry once it can judge the whole log (R43). A save refused because a case lock file, the gate or the case lock, was removed or replaced says so, says that the save was not recorded, and asks the underwriter to reload and save again. So does a save refused because the log changed since its check or while it was being recorded, or because another save is under way in the same window (R42). A save whose read-back failed says the save was not recorded; one whose head record was not then taken back says that whether the save is in the log is not confirmed, names what stopped the take-back, and asks the underwriter to check the case before saving again (R42 since revision 17, D143); no part of that message, nor any error it carries, says the save was recorded, was not recorded or that nothing was written (R42 since revision 21, D164, D168). A save that met an operating-system error names it and says that the save was not recorded or, when its head record was published and not taken back, that whether it is in the log is not confirmed (R42 since revision 18, D153; one rule since revision 19, D158). A save refused partway through its line says that part of its entry was written and that the save was not recorded (R42 since revision 19). Once any part of a save's line is in the log, no message about the save says nothing was written. A save whose own check could not read the log, the head record or a lock file names that error, and never asks the underwriter to reload or says a lock file was removed or replaced (R42 since revision 20).
635:| R42, R43 | C3 (log, head record written before the first line, the gate and the case lock, concurrency scope, one save at a time per holder, what a reader reports, a check's shared hold, damage never dropped, the re-check of both lock files before each step a reader reads, the first head record created and never renamed over, the read-back before a save is told recorded and the take-back when it fails, with the not-confirmed answer when it is not made, the not-recorded and not-confirmed answers, naming the error, when the file system fails a step or a read the save makes to check, the not-recorded answer for a save refused partway through its line, no report saying nothing was written once the line is in the log, one answer per report, its causes included, with a not-confirmed report naming what stopped its take-back in the save's own words, the head record's publication known from its rename, a check that cannot read named as its error and never as a change or a lock file removed or replaced, the repair's re-check, a reader's read taken again through the gate and the case lock across a changed or removed lock file, a holder's and a busy-gate read included, the check after a read that looks at each lock file and then the first again, a holder's also at whether the files it holds still have a name, the window after a check's last look, the lock files as the tool's own), C6 (sign-off judges the whole log holding the case), C5 (one process, no forked workers; being saved and a refused save shown as such) |
662:- Case folders on a file system without hard links, such as FAT or exFAT (revision 15). The first append links its empty head record into place, which fails there; the save writes nothing and, since revision 18, answers not recorded, naming the file system's error (R42); the README and the trial sheet name the file systems to use.
688:- R5 and R6 say the precedence the categoriser follows (revisions 8 and 9), so T05 is judged on text that matches T03's build: a refund, a loan or a returned payment that names a director or an own account keeps what its wording says, and a credit both kinds claim is ambiguous. Since revision 11 R4 likewise says how the bank's code and a pattern's alternatives meet the longer-phrase rule, so T03 is judged on text that matches D100's build. Since revision 12 R42, R43 and P15 do the same for T12: they name the steps a writer re-checks its lock before (D108), and say that a reader that cannot know one writer held the case through its read names only a fault at or before the recorded entries (D109). Since revision 13 they also say what a save is told under lock-file interference, and run one save at a time per holder (D124), so T12 is judged on text that names the repair boundary. Since revision 14 R42 says when a save is told recorded and what a repair re-checks (D125), and R4 that cash wording is never hidden inside a payment-method phrase and that no v2 rule carries an exception (D126, D127), so both tasks are judged on text that says what 1d16873, 1b592b6 and 4adddf9 build. Since revision 15 R4 says that cash paid-in wording and a payment-method cue never take each other in and that a lookaround is an exception (D132, D136), and R42 that the re-check covers the gate (D136), so both tasks are judged on text that says what 78556a7 and cd298cc build. Since revision 16 R4 says that an anchor is refused except on the bank's code and that only a match that itself counts sets another aside, and R42, R43 and P15 that a failed read-back takes its head record back and that a read across a changed lock file is taken again (D137, D141), so both tasks are judged on text that says what 5c9a5bd, 2c72469 and f5436bb build. Since revision 17 R4 says that the set-aside rule is judged match by match (D142). R42 and P15 say that a save whose take-back is not made is not confirmed (D143). R43 and P15 say that a holder's and a busy-gate read count only while their lock files are the case's, and that interference before a read is outside what a reader can know (D144). So both tasks are judged on text that says what 4b3b21c, 0d339cc and 3076547 build, apart from the take-back's lock re-check, which revision 17 keeps and D149 confirms. Since revision 18 R42 says how a save answers when the file system fails one of its steps, R43 that a read taken again goes through the locks, and R4 that only the pinned v1 table is spared the table checks (D153), so both tasks are judged on text that says what 7c8fba2 and 9042fb4 build, apart from the file system's errors at a save's other steps, which R42 now reaches and revision 18 asks about. Since revision 19 R42 and P15 say that a save refused partway through its line is not recorded, never nothing written, and R42 states one rule for every operating-system error a save meets after its check; R4 and R54 say that a changed v1 is refused as changed before anything else is checked, and R4 that a malformed value is named by rule (D158). So both tasks are judged on text that says what 9c265a3, 51c8b68 and 7cb2e71 build, apart from the reads a save makes only to check, which revision 19 asks about, and the one validator path in 7cb2e71 that still raises a type error. Since revision 20 R42 names the reads a save makes only to check. R42 and P15 say that no part of a save's report says nothing was written once its line is in the log. R4 says that a malformed value on a bank-code rule is named by its rule, and that any failure of a changed v1 is named as changed first (D163). So both tasks are judged on text that says what de6a56e, 1e35352 and 5139e22 build, apart from the words a not-confirmed report quotes, which revision 20 asks about. Since revision 21 R42 says that each report gives one answer, its causes included, and that whether a head record was published is known from its rename, and R4 that a changed v1 names its exceptions and that every rule's matchers are built while the table is checked (D168). So both tasks are judged on text that says what 26bc51e, 7fb3a23 and 5f5bb2e build, apart from the refusal before any part of a save's line, which 26bc51e words as a refused step, and an unusable pattern beside another malformed field, which 5f5bb2e does not yet name. Since revision 22 R43 and P15 say how a reader's check looks at the lock files and what no check can see. R4 says that only a matching digest spares v1, and that a key given more than once is named with every value checked (D192). So both tasks are judged on text that says what 523c5df and 4c017d4 build, apart from the refusal before any part of a save's line, which 523c5df still words as a refused step, and the values of a repeated field that 4c017d4 does not yet check.
690:- A table edit cannot give payer wording the bank's standing (D96, D97), a table that would fail on a credit fails when it loads instead (D94), and since D110 a pattern cannot reach across words to hide another list. Since D115 to D121 a table edit cannot switch the bank's code off, a pattern whose longest match cannot be found or that matches no text is refused, and a rule id cannot blur a line's provenance. Since D126 no rule can carry an exception that hides another list's wording, and since D136 and D137 no pattern can carry one as a lookahead, a lookbehind or an anchor. Since D153 a table cannot skip those checks by calling itself frozen: only v1's pinned bytes are frozen. Since D158 a changed v1 is refused as changed before anything else in it is checked, and no malformed value is to escape as a type error. Since D163 that holds whatever else a malformed rule carries and whatever a change to v1 makes fail. Since D168 a changed v1 names the exceptions it gained, and a pattern that cannot be used is named with the table's other faults. Since D192 nothing spares a table but a digest of its bytes that matches the pin, and a key given twice is named while every value it was given is still checked, so a repeat hides no fault.
692:- The case log has a stated boundary (D65, D99) and a reader that claims only what it saw held still (D74). The reader says intact only after finding the case free (revision 7). Since revision 10 it knows from the gate, not from a mark or a count, that the writer it found held the case through its read (D95), so the lock files hold no data. A writer refuses once it sees either lock file gone or replaced (the gate since revision 15), and two saves told recorded at one position under interference show as a broken log, so the review judges the lock against R42's text instead of searching for the next race between a check and a write. The fork machinery (D63), the lock-file marks (D69) and the hold counts (D93) are gone. Since revision 13 the boundary is stated by what a writer is told: a save told recorded is never missing from a log that reads intact, a save refused after its line says so, and a second save on one thread is refused (D124). Since revision 14 R42 also says how a writer knows: it reads back what it published before saying so (D125). Since revision 16 a save told not recorded leaves its entry where the next holder can drop it, and a reader re-reads when a lock file it holds changes under it (D141). Since revision 17 a save whose head record is not taken back says it is not confirmed instead of not recorded, so the tool never tells a person a save was not recorded while the log holds it (D143). Since revision 18 a save the file system fails answers too, naming the error, so a caller always hears recorded, not recorded or not confirmed (D153). Since revision 19 a save refused partway through its line says it was not recorded, never that nothing was written, so a person is never told a case is untouched when it needs a repair (D158). Since revision 20 no part of a save's report says that, whatever stopped the save. A save that cannot read what it checks names the error, never another window's save or a removed lock file (D159, D163). Since revision 21 each report gives one answer, its causes included, so a person told a save is not confirmed is never also told it was or was not recorded, and a save whose head record cannot be read before its rename is told it was not recorded, never not confirmed (D164, D168). Since revision 22 R43 and P15 state what a reader's check of its lock files can see. A review then judges the check against a stated window instead of moving the replacement past whichever look comes last (D192).
715:- The first empty head record is linked into place (revision 15, after verify-68), so a case folder needs a file system with hard links. NTFS, APFS and the usual Linux file systems have them; FAT and exFAT, common on USB sticks, do not, and there the first append writes nothing and answers not recorded, naming the file system's error (R42 since revision 18). The README and the trial sheet name the file systems to use.
716:- More re-checks (D108; R42 since revision 12; the gate since revision 15): a writer checks both lock files before every write call and every rename attempt of a step a reader reads, each one file status call. A step refused after the line leaves an unrecorded entry, which the next holder drops, and the window between each check and its step remains. Since D125 (R42 since revision 14) a save also reads back the log and the head record after publishing, and a repair re-checks the log and the head record before its log rename and the head record before its head rename: a few reads of short files per save. Since cd298cc an append and a repair also read the whole log to compare its bytes with their check, a read that grows with the log; a case log holds a few hundred short entries. A repair refused after its log rename leaves a broken log to resolve by hand. A save whose read-back fails takes back its head record (revision 16), one more rename, so its entry is past the recorded entries for the next holder to drop; one whose take-back is not made is reported as not confirmed (D143), and `cma case check` shows whether its entry is among the recorded entries. A reader also compares each lock file it holds with its path after each read (revision 16), one file status call each. These happen only under interference or a read error. Since revision 18 a save the file system fails answers not recorded or, when its head record cannot be made durable, not confirmed, and the person checks the case log before saving again. Since revision 19 a step refused partway through the line answers not recorded as well, and since revision 20 (D159, D163) a read a save makes only to check names its error instead of answering a conflict.
728:- **Staff.** *Smallest change:* Tier 1 is the smallest set that shows the mechanism in the reply's terms; Tier 2 can be cut off. The cash change is one category mapped into an existing set, and the policy check reuses policy.evaluate and the guard as they are. The ambiguity policy replaces per-pair exceptions with one verdict and keeps revision 2's precedence, inserting that verdict between the protected verdict and firm rules. Revision 4 made R4 smaller: the queue it named belongs to R15. Revision 5 wrote down the case log's scope, which let D63 remove the fork machinery. Revision 6 made the reader claim less and removed the lock-file marks; in C1 it replaced an exception and per-list spellings with one cue and one separator set. Revision 7 moved the profile name one step down the precedence and made the reader try the lock before it says intact; neither added a component. Revision 8 wrote the existing precedence into R5 and R6 and added nothing to the build. Revision 9 moved one case, a credit both profile kinds match, from step (5) to step (4), and kept R5 and R6 clear of the queue. Revision 10 writes down a scope for the lock files instead of adding machinery, and takes in the gate, which replaced the probe after the read and the hold counts; it adds no component. Revision 11 writes D101 and D102 into R4, which D100 already builds, and adds nothing to the build but one validator check (D110) and a few re-checks (D108). Revision 12 writes D108 and D109 into R42, R43 and P15: T12's reader already does what they say, and the worktree's uncommitted work does the re-checks, so the only build change it asks for is the message after a step refused past the line. Revision 13 writes the repair boundary and one save at a time into R42 and P15, which b428d56 half builds, and adds a read-back per save and a re-check per repair rename to close the corner a repair still leaves; in C1 its default removes a schema feature, exceptions, instead of adding one; it adds no component. Revision 14 writes D125 to D127 into R4 and R42, which 1b592b6, 4adddf9 and 1d16873 already build; its one default makes one condition in T03's longer-phrase filter two-way; it adds no component. Revision 15 writes D132 and D136 into R4 and R42, which 78556a7 and cd298cc already build; its one default adds one validator check in T03, for anchors; it adds no component. Revision 16 writes D137 and D141 into R4, R42, R43 and P15, which 5c9a5bd, 2c72469 and f5436bb already build; its defaults ask T03 to settle matches by length instead of rules in rounds, T12 to compare one more read and to give a failed take-back its own answer, and R43 and P15 to state one more limit; it adds no component. Revision 17 writes D142 to D144 into R4, R42, R43 and P15, which 4b3b21c, 0d339cc and 3076547 build. It keeps the lock re-check on the take-back, which asks T12 to turn one check back on, and it adds no component. Revision 18 writes D149 and D153 into R4, R42 and R43, which 9042fb4 and 7c8fba2 build; its default reads R42's answer for a save the file system fails across every step of the save, which asks T12 to map four more call sites, and it adds no component. Revision 19 writes D154 and D158 into R4, R42, R54 and P15, which 9c265a3, 51c8b68 and 7cb2e71 build; its default reads R42's naming of the error into the reads a save makes only to check, which asks T12 to carry the error out of six reads, and it adds no component. Revision 20 writes D159 and D163 into R4, R42 and P15, which de6a56e, 1e35352 and 5139e22 build; its default asks T12 to word two refusals for the take-back, and it adds no component. Revision 21 writes D164 and D168 into R4 and R42, which 26bc51e, 7fb3a23 and 5f5bb2e build; it asks T12 to word one refusal for the save and T03 to build a pattern's forms beside a malformed field, and it adds no component. Revision 22 writes D192 into R4, R43 and P15, which 523c5df and 4c017d4 build. It asks T03 to run the checks it already has over every value of a repeated field, whichever comes first, and it adds no component. *Ownership:* arithmetic and the queue's assembly stay in metrics.py and indicators.py, the outcome in policy.py, categorisation in categorise.py plus the rule data, the log and its lock files in case.py; the workspace and pack only render. *Six-month misunderstandings removed:*
740:  - A save is recorded when its writer says so, not when its line is on disk: a save refused after its line leaves an entry the next holder may drop, and a log without it is intact (D124). A writer says so only after reading back an intact log that holds the entry (D125), and a save whose read-back fails takes its head record back, so its entry is past the recorded entries (revision 16), and one whose take-back is not made is not confirmed, which a check of the case log settles (revision 17). A save the file system fails answers as well, never with the operating system's error: not recorded before its head record's rename, not confirmed after it unless the head record was taken back (revisions 18 and 19). A save refused partway through its line is not recorded, never nothing written (revision 19). Each report gives one answer, its causes included: a save not confirmed is never also told it was or was not recorded, and whether its head record was published is known from the rename, not from reading the head record back (revision 21).
742:  *Most likely wrong:* the memo fields (E8), the cash default (D31) and limits (D35), the category order inside a bucket, the separator set's reach across words, the protected verdict above a profile name, which files JANE DOE LOAN as lending, the both-kinds rule, which queues a director's own company, the assumption that the firm keeps working files on a local disk, the assumption that nothing in the firm's setup cleans or restores case folders, D109's answer for a check that meets a writer at the gate while another holds the case, which claims nothing where a reader might expect the recorded entries, and D124's boundary, which lets a confirmed drop remove a save told it was not recorded, D142's set-aside rule judged by match, D143's not-confirmed answer, D149's take-back that re-checks the lock files, which answers not confirmed more often under interference, revision 18's default that R42's answer for a save the file system fails reaches every step of the save, D159's naming of the error in the reads a save makes only to check, D164's not-confirmed report, which gives one answer and quotes no other, revision 21's reading that a changed v1 still calling itself frozen is not held to the checks only the maintained lists need, and revision 22's reading of D192's 'finished within the check's own last look' as a change made after the check's last look at that file. *Out of scope:* listed above.
749:  - the case log's exclusion and reader cases run as P15 tests. These include a writer paused at the gate, alone and while another holds the case, and a holder that lets go during a read. They also include the interference cases: a lock file replaced before each step a reader reads (the empty head record, the line, each rename; refused, nothing further published), and replaced after a re-check (two saves at one position, reported broken, never intact). Since revision 13 they include one save at a time (a save begun inside another on the same thread, and a line added after a save's check; b428d56 has both) and the repair schedules: verify-64's, which leaves an intact log and a save told not recorded; verify-66's two, an append and a repair at one position and a repair after the log grew; and a save whose head record lands between a repair's log rename and its head rename, which ends broken or with the save told not recorded, never intact with a save told recorded missing. 1d16873 had four of these. Since revision 15 they also include the gate removed or replaced during a save, a first append from a stale check that never resets a head record, and a repair refused when the log was rewritten to the same length (verify-68). cd298cc has all three; the gate's test is named p15, and none of the eight tests added since review 20 yet is. Since revision 16 they also include a read across a replaced gate or case lock that is taken again (f5436bb, named p15) and a failed read-back that takes its head record back (f5436bb, not yet named p15); since revision 17 they include a busy-gate read and a holder's read across a replaced case lock (0d339cc, both named p15) and a take-back that is not made, answering not confirmed (3076547, not yet named p15). As the revision 17 default they also include a take-back that a replaced lock file stops, answering not confirmed, and the schedule in which a take-back without that re-check would unrecord a repair told recorded. 0d339cc's test that a take-back goes through a replaced lock file (tests/test_case_log.py:2401) changed with that default, and at 7c8fba2 it expects not confirmed. Since revision 18 they include a head record that cannot be made durable, answering not confirmed, and a busy-gate read whose case lock is removed during it, which looks again (7c8fba2, the second named p15), and a failure by the file system at the line, the first empty head record and a repair's rename, answering not recorded and naming the error (51c8b68, not yet named p15). Since revision 19 they include a lock lost after part of the line is written, answering not recorded (9c265a3, not yet named p15). Since revision 20 they include a lock lost after the whole line, whose report never says nothing was written (de6a56e). They also include a read error injected into the log comparison, the head record's re-check, the read-back, the lock files' examination and a repair's re-check, answering with the error named and never as a change (1e35352). Neither is named p15 yet, and the second accepts either answer at every site. Since revision 21 they include a take-back stopped by a replaced lock file or by a head record no longer the save's own, whose not-confirmed report, causes included, never says the save was or was not recorded or that nothing was written; a lock lost after the whole line whose causes never say nothing was written; and a head record that cannot be read before its rename, answering not recorded (26bc51e and 7fb3a23; none named p15 yet). They should also include a save refused before any part of its line, whose report says it was not recorded. Since revision 22 they include a lock file replaced just after its path was looked at, and a gate moved aside and replaced after its first look (523c5df, both named p15).
751:- **Data-intensive.** *Idempotency:* imports are keyed by file hash, an identical override changes nothing, and a second sign-off is refused. *Determinism:* a credit's table verdict, category and table candidates are a pure function of its description, the sign of its amount and the tables with their category order. Its profile candidates, a profile settlement and a profile-made ambiguity add the case profile's names, which the snapshot hashes. Neither a credit's bucket nor its category depends on rule order within a stage or, since revision 10, on the order of alternatives inside a pattern; only which of several same-category rules is recorded, and the order candidates are written in, follow table order. A debit's category follows table order by design. *Ordering and concurrency:* one writer at a time per case, across windows, commands, threads and CaseLog objects in one process, on a local disk, while nobody removes or replaces the lock files, and one save at a time per holder; enforced by a gate, a lock, a save-in-progress mark and the expected log position. A second tab is refused while the first writes, and gets a conflict and reloads if it writes from a stale position. A reader holds the gate shared while it reads, so no writer takes the case during the read; it holds the case lock shared too when the case is free, and a writer waits for it. While another writer holds the case, a reader judges only the recorded entries, from a read the head record held still across. *Partial failure:* append-only logs with a check for a torn last entry; the head record is written before the first line, so a lost head record is never mistaken for a torn first append; a repair replaces the log in one rename; the pack is written to a temporary folder and then renamed. Two saves told recorded at one position under interference break the chain or the head record there. A step refused once any part of the line is written leaves an unrecorded entry, whole or torn, as a crash does, and the writer says the save was not recorded, never that nothing was written (revision 19). A step the file system fails answers the same way, naming the error, and one after the head record's rename, which cannot be made durable, answers not confirmed (revisions 18 and 19). *Consistency:* the workspace reads the latest state; the preview and sign-off bind to a snapshot that includes the ruleset, the profile, thresholds, the policy hash and the show_policy_verdict setting, so a sign-off cannot attach to an outcome the underwriter did not see. *Migration:* additive, nothing to backfill. *Money:* integer pence, GBP only, thresholds compared in pence, and amounts with more than two decimals are refused.
792:Reconciliation: M17 = M2 + M19 + M20 + M21 + M22 + M23 + M24 + M25, in pence. M18 is part of M2, is not added again, and its evidence is a subset of M2's. M16 and M26 share one denominator: money in less lending drawdowns, returned direct debits and own-account transfers. Cash deposits are trading receipts, so they are in neither numerator; an ambiguous credit is unexplained, so it is in both, whatever profile name it carries as a candidate. Credits that fall to OTHER today keep the same treatment in M9 whichever new bucket they land in, with one exception: cash deposits now count as trading cash (D31). No committed fixture or synthetic case has a cash line (Consumer trace, item 13), so no committed value moves because of cash. M27 averages the closing balance of every calendar day in the period, carried over days with no lines, as M6 does. M28 adds the proposed monthly repayment for each month of the period to existing debt service.
859:- A save tells its caller it was recorded only after reading back, once its head record is published, an intact log that holds its entry where that head record lists it, and it publishes its head record only if the head record still lists what its check read. A repair re-checks before its log rename that the log, byte for byte, and the head record are as its check read them, and before its head rename that the head record is (R42 since revision 14; D125). A save whose read-back fails takes back its head record, when that record is still its own, leaving its entry past the recorded entries, and says the save was not recorded (R42 since revision 16); one whose take-back is not made, because its head record is no longer its own, a lock file was removed or replaced, or the head record cannot be read or renamed, says the save is not confirmed and asks for the case log to be checked (D143; R42 since revision 17). The take-back re-checks both lock files and the head record before its rename, as every head rename does (D149; R42 since revision 17). A save that meets an operating-system error after its check, in a step or in a read it makes to re-check or read itself back, answers, naming the error, never with the error itself: not recorded, unless its head record was published and not taken back, when it answers not confirmed and asks for the case log to be checked (R42 since revision 18; one rule since revision 19). Its reads to check something are among those steps. One that cannot be made is named as its error, never answered as a conflict or as a lock file removed or replaced, and a lock file missing when examined was removed (R42 since revision 20). Whether its head record's rename was made is known from the rename, never read back from a head record that may be unreadable, so a head record that cannot be read before its rename answers not recorded. Each report gives one answer, its causes included: no part of a not-recorded report says the save was recorded or is not confirmed, and a not-confirmed report names what stopped its take-back in the save's own words, and no part of it says the save was or was not recorded or that nothing was written (R42 since revision 21; D164, D168).
866:  5. when the lock cannot be taken or tested and no read held still, not checked, with the lock error named and nothing claimed.
879:The case summary carries the case log's state as one of the case log contract's answers, so a second window says the case is being saved rather than warning of a broken log. A line in the judgement queue carries its rule and, for an ambiguous credit, its candidates, each with the category its rule or profile kind sets and the bucket that category maps to, or no bucket for a payment-method cue (R15). A line in a drill-down carries its rule as recorded (R16). The firm-rule preview counts the lines the rule matches and will not apply to, because a protected or ambiguous verdict, or a profile name, settled them (R20, R21, R4). Every request carries the session token. Every write carries the expected log position, gets a conflict if that position is stale, and is refused while another writer holds the case, when another save is under way in the same window, when the log changed since its check, or when a case lock file, the gate or the case lock, was removed or replaced. A write whose read-back does not find its entry is reported as not recorded (R42 since revision 14), and its head record is taken back when it is still the write's own (R42 since revision 16); one whose take-back is not made is reported as not confirmed (R42 since revision 17). A write that meets an operating-system error is reported, naming the error, as not recorded or, when its head record was published and not taken back, as not confirmed (R42 since revision 18; one rule since revision 19), and a write refused partway through its line as not recorded (R42 since revision 19). No report about a write whose line is in the log says nothing was written, and a write whose own check could not read names that error (R42 since revision 20). Each report gives one answer: a not-confirmed report names what stopped the take-back and says only that whether the write is in the log is not confirmed, never that it was or was not recorded (R42 since revision 21).
930:| A rule's pattern is given as a list, a mapping or a number (pattern: [CASH DEPOSIT] beside unless: FPS on the cash rule; verify-77) | Refused at load, naming the rule and the field, with the table's other faults, never raised as a type error (D158; R4 since revision 19; 7cb2e71). At 9042fb4 the bank-code membership test raised a TypeError before either fault was named. At 7cb2e71 a bank-code rule whose pattern was a list still raised one where the codes the bank-code rules carry are gathered (src/cma/categorise.py:534 there; verify-79, with the returned-payment rule's pattern as [UNPAID DD]). 5139e22 closes it (:534-535), and R4 says since revision 20 that such a rule is named whatever else it carries, the bank-code flag included (D163). A failure no check foresees is still refused, as a malformed value that could not be checked, but names no rule (:886-892 at 4c017d4) | R4 |
998:| The lock cannot be taken or tested (an error that is not another writer holding it) | Not another holder: a reader believes a steady read as it stands, a torn entry included; with no steady read it reports that the log could not be checked because the lock could not be tested, names no other window, and `cma case check` exits 4; a writer's append fails with the error and writes nothing, since taking the case comes before the save's check and R42's answers for a failed step cover the steps after it | R43, P15 |
1012:| A save's read-back fails after its head record is published (a read error, or a log a repair rewrote under interference; verify-70) | The save takes back the head record it published, when it is still its own, by putting back the head record its check read; the save is told not recorded, its entry is past the recorded entries, and the next holder confirms dropping it (D141; R42 since revision 16; f5436bb). A head record another writer published since is left alone. At cd298cc the head record stayed, a fresh check read the log intact with the entry, and the next holder had nothing to drop | R42, P15 |
1014:| The folder that holds a save's head record cannot be synced after the head record's rename (an I/O error; verify-74) | The head record lists the save but may not last a power cut, so the save is not confirmed, names the error and asks for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved; it makes no read-back or take-back (D153; R42 since revision 18; 7c8fba2). At 3076547 the operating system's error reached the caller while the head record listed the save | R42 |
1015:| The file system fails another step of a save after its check (a full disk while the line is written, an I/O error syncing it, a repair's staging file or log rename) | The save is not recorded and names the error; any part of its line already written stays past the recorded entries, as a crash leaves it, for the next holder to drop (R42 since revision 18; every step, D154). 51c8b68 maps it at every step that changes a file (_os_errors_as_not_recorded, src/cma/case.py:842-852) | R42 |
1016:| A read the save makes only to check fails (an I/O error reading the log for the byte comparison before the line, the head record before its rename or the log for the read-back; a lock file that cannot be examined) | The save names the error and is not recorded, or not confirmed in a take-back. A re-check that cannot read is never answered as a conflict, and a lock file that cannot be examined never as removed or replaced. A read-back that cannot read is a failed read-back, and is taken back (D159; R42 since revision 19, read by read since revision 20, D163). 1e35352 builds it (src/cma/case.py:793-808, :957-963, :1001, :1076-1085). At 51c8b68 the byte comparison and a repair's re-check refused with a conflict that asks to reload, the head record's re-check said the head file changed, the read-back said the log changed, and a lock file that could not be examined read as removed or replaced | R42 |
